window.harnessChunks["rows-bafe67706c92ff4d.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T11:31:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Maestro</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/tree/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165\">6c8a592 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute independently scoped project tasks and produce model-chosen code, research or operational output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source changes, requirements, test failures and external messages for an agent&#39;s owned repository/process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the active external CLI model decides what tools to use and how to revise its work. - Decision owner: attached Claude Code/Codex/other supported CLI model agent; AI Maestro supplies runtime access, message and context transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tmux/remote session management, per-agent clone, tasks, AMP, memory and tool approvals. - Closure path: operator/task brief → model agent session acts in own working directory → changed artifact/test result → agent reports via message/task board. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the dashboard can display/send work but does not generate the actual task-specific code decisions without its model agent. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/README.md; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/agent-runtime.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-core-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-chat-service.ts - Basis: shipped CLI integration and task communication path; no live external LLM E2E. - Confidence: high for supported S1 actor boundary, unmeasured outcomes. - Caveats: some supported terminal programs have fewer chat/control integrations; no third-party provider internals are credited. - Boundary reachability: standard deployed agent session on local tmux or supported remote host.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no qualifying first-party inter-S1 incompatible work arbitration or repair path was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two independent agents may change the same API or repository branch incompatibly. - Decisive decision or feedback right: humans/agents choose how to respond to AMP messages; the service only delivers messages and tracks task blockers. - Decision owner: no deployed S2 controller with a specific conflict-detection/settlement right demonstrated; agents retain local task choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent clone/branch isolation, signed AMP transport, meeting delivery statuses, blockedBy graph and waiting cards. - Closure path: agents see messages/blocked status and may respond; no shipped concrete incompatible-artifact detection and corrective return to each S1 was verified. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AMP, org/task links and Git clone separation transport or prevent some overlap but do not prove actual cross-S1 correction judgment. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-messaging-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/meeting-broadcast-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/task-registry.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/docs/CONCEPTS.md - Basis: bounded negative across message, task dependency and Git ownership surfaces. - Confidence: medium; human or agent teams may build effective coordination practices outside this tested first-party circuit. - Caveats: dependency completion computes newly unblocked IDs but does not itself merge incompatible code, enforce dependent execution or negotiate changed interfaces.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: let a parent human regulate present distributed team work, current priorities, assignments and blocked commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: many agents across hosts, blocked or overdue tasks, limited operator attention, delivery failures and work waiting for approval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator chooses which agent owns a card, current priority/status, instructions, meeting scope and approval response from team-wide present state. - Decision owner: parent human operator acting through installed dashboard and chat, not a default AI Maestro management model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: host/agent status aggregation, Matrix/Attention board, task API, chat-to-terminal delivery, team meeting and receipt/status records. - Closure path: team-wide active/blocked/waiting view → human assignment or intervention → persisted task update or message/approval delivery → agent receives new current commitment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: UI and API do not choose priorities or reconcile across team autonomously; present controlling judgment remains with user (P). - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/organization.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agent-tasks-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-chat-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/meeting-broadcast-service.ts - Basis: supported parent-assisted operating mode with real whole-team view and task/message write paths. - Confidence: medium-high for available human-controlled current-team closure; individual user&#39;s actual meeting choices are not observed. - Caveats: no automatic model S3 is claimed; unattended API usage alone and task board visuals alone do not prove the human actually exercises every S3 judgment. - Whole-system current view: Matrix/Attention aggregates open, waiting, review and blocked cards across agents and hosts; organization view groups active work and assignments. - Current-control decision scope: assign/change task owner, choose present priority and current commitments, send corrective instruction or answer a pending agent approval. - Boundary reachability: normal self-hosted dashboard and integrated task/chat API directly expose operator write actions to working agent sessions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party sufficiently independent alternative audit of work outcomes and return to control was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker could falsely report completion or produce a regression unobserved by routine status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator may review a card/commit trace, but no separately invoked independent challenger is mandated or shown. - Decision owner: no specific S3 judge for actual work evidence within assessed code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review column, commit trace, agent chat history, docs/code graph and activity status. - Closure path: visible trace can lead a human to inspect manually, without a packaged complementary auditor diagnosis and correction route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: passive trace, status and ordinary review labels do not choose adverse fault findings. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/README.md; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/commit-trace-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agent-tasks-service.ts - Basis: bounded negative across review statuses, session tracing and docs/code index. - Confidence: medium; an external agent role could provide audit when explicitly installed elsewhere. - Caveats: a screenshot of a code-reviewer agent is not proof of its executable independent audit or return loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective environmental options process was wired back into the operator&#39;s present team capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future customers, infrastructure changes, agent models and emerging threats may require adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: local memory and scheduled agent tasks collect/contextualize history; no S4 options owner is shown. - Decision owner: human leadership for future strategy, if supplied outside this app. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: long-term memory, entity graph, agent schedules, code graph and reusable notes. - Closure path: learned retrospective context reaches later prompts, but no external prospective option/choice → S3 capability change loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory consolidation and nightly summaries are retrospective operational support, not outside-and-then policy intelligence. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/memory/consolidate.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-schedule-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/README.md - Basis: bounded negative on memory, schedules, organization and information surfaces. - Confidence: medium-high at published runtime boundary. - Caveats: an operator can program a strategic research agent; that external configuration is not first-party S4 provenance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity-level ultimate policy court or balance of current and future organizational identity is implemented. - Disturbance / variety regulated: company labels, agent roles, secret permissions, approval and conflicting objectives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator can label organizations and approve individual secrets/actions; there is no identity-level dispute resolution path. - Decision owner: human company authority external to installed model worker ensemble; no evidenced packaged S5 issue closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: org tags and names, agent roles, permission/secret UI, messages and dashboard. - Closure path: task approval or org rename affects local work/display, but no ultimate policy ruling is returned to a sustained S3/S4 decision relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an organization record is explicitly shown, not enforced; a role label or approval does not constitute S5. - Evidence: https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/lib/organization.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/orgs-service.ts; https://github.com/23blocks-OS/ai-maestro/blob/6c8a5927d66637855c2a31d1b54f92abc9b9e165/services/agents-chat-service.ts - Basis: bounded negative review of org, identity, role, permission and approval surfaces. - Confidence: high that labels alone are not governance; external human policy remains outside boundary. - Caveats: actual human-run companies may have real S5, but they are not inherited by this software product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/57b15cceb7ca319c1f0aa55399f182700a3c1255/assessments/ai/ai-maestro.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 57b15cceb7ca319c1f0aa55399f182700a3c1255\">57b15cc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T00:59:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shareAI-lab/kode-agent-sdk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KODE SDK</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shareAI-lab/kode-agent-sdk/tree/2596118805d8968c7e33cc437e440721833e9689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2596118805d8968c7e33cc437e440721833e9689\">2596118 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute a supplied task through an autonomous model-backed operational actor using offered tools and feedback from their effects. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable user input, sandbox contents, tool errors, permission outcomes and intermediate model responses require choosing what to do next. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The external model selects a next tool call or content response using the context and advertised schemas; subsequent tool observations influence the next model request. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed Agent operational work cell. The model provider supplies inference; first-party SDK code deliberately constructs and closes its model/tool/observation loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry/runner, PermissionManager, sandbox, context compression, stores, message queue, hooks, events, scheduling and operator-configured limits. - Closure path: Agent.complete/chat → queued prompt → runStep → model.stream → tool-use selection → executeTools → tool outcomes appended as user messages → ensureProcessing/next model turn or completed answer. - Boundary reachability: Agent is a public SDK export and its normal complete mode directly invokes the agent loop; no example-only controller or evaluation job is needed to receive and act on model-chosen tool calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without model discretion, the existing deterministic transport, retries, approvals and tool runner cannot decide which task-specific tool to invoke next. The model&#39;s tool selection is operational ownership, not merely its ability to produce text. - Evidence: public Agent export; complete and send; stream and closure; delegated actor completion. - Basis: Structural first-party runtime call path and published API. - Confidence: High for implemented autonomous decision/feedback; no empirical claim of effectiveness or safety. - Caveats: The SDK requires external model credentials/dependencies and user-provided task/template. A child Agent is a bounded operational unit, not automatic proof of recursive viability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party coordination circuit specifically attenuating interference, collision or oscillation between two distinct S1 work cells is evidenced in the standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Separate agents could potentially disagree, duplicate edits or collide in one sandbox, but the SDK does not represent a concrete shared-work conflict and a conflict-specific response. - Distinct S1 units: AgentPool holds multiple independent model-backed Agent instances, and Room delivers messages to selected members; this establishes multiplicity, not S2 closure. - Inter-S1 disturbance: No collision detection, reservation ownership, contradictory-output reconciliation, conflict arbitration or feedback from a detected peer disturbance is represented in the reviewed multi-agent primitives. - Attenuating coordination relation: Room.say transfers text to one or more peers; taskrun serially delegates a parent task to a child. Neither checks or dampens any particular inter-agent interference. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Recipient models may condition their next output on delivered messages, but there is no coordination result from an inter-S1 instability being fed back under a first-party S2 protocol. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The functions inspected are precisely ordinary transport, pool lifecycle and delegation. An embedding app could add a dispute procedure, but none is installed by KODE SDK itself. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developers/operators decide topology and communication content; no first-party S2 response-selection or conflict-resolution owner is established. - Decision owner: No qualifying S2 owner inside this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Room membership map, mentions/broadcast, pool capacity checks, subagent depth/template restrictions, caller-defined coordinator system prompt. - Closure path: Parent invokes child and awaits result, or Room.say calls peer complete; neither closes a specifically evidenced conflict → mitigation → revised S1 behavior relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent-originated task messages may be discretionary, but autonomy of message content does not make generic delivery an S2 function. - Evidence: Room; pool; taskrun tool; documented pattern is user-authored. - Basis: Bounded negative on shipped first-party multi-agent path. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An application&#39;s own scripted negotiation, locking or conflict-aware shared-work layer would move the assessed boundary; no such application is included here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No bundled whole-current regulation of the active agent population&#39;s conflicting commitments, shared-resource allocation or accountability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agents could exhaust concurrent capacity, exceed context limits or produce weak work, but the SDK exposes fixed caps and local agent processes, not a controller that evaluates and revises the whole&#39;s priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The embedding developer chooses which agents to create and how to distribute tasks; AgentPool.maxAgents is a configured guard, not a model-owned or constructor-managed choice among current commitments. - Decision owner: External embedding application/developer for population design; the SDK has no qualified S3 owner or parent-return circuit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pool maxAgents, task delegation, per-agent todos, context compression, step scheduler, event monitor, graceful shutdown and resume. - Closure path: Fixed configured limits reject additional creations; shutdown persists/interrupts operations; subagent results return to their parent task. No whole-population appraisal → discretionary resource/priorities revision → changed S1 operations exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A coordinator-like prompt in docs may choose delegates inside its own task; without an installed whole-current view and authority over the other S1 units, delegation is S1 decomposition rather than S3. Hard-coded capacity cannot own choices about commitments. - Evidence: pool create and maxAgents; pool shutdown; local scheduler; parent task delegation. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking S3 constructor and parent possibilities. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A self-hosted app could place an autonomous manager over this SDK; that manager is not a first-party supported control mode established in the package.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent inspection of claims by operating agents beyond their ordinary output/tool/event stream is part of this SDK&#39;s working governance loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent results might be inaccurate, unauthorized or misleading; permission checks and event logs are local execution governance rather than independent raw-reality audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separately authorized audit finding about operational reports is made from complementary evidence and fed into current-control decisions. - Decision owner: Operator may inspect events or build a reviewer agent in an application; the SDK itself does not construct independent audit ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Progress/control/monitor event channels, approval decisions, tool-result records, generic reviewer template documented in examples, snapshot/replay facilities. - Closure path: Tool results reenter the same producer&#39;s model context; event subscribers and human approvals can affect routine work but have no independent audit judgment returning to an S3 regulator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second model named reviewer in a user template is normal production QA until the independently sourced evidence, auditor ownership and return to separate control are demonstrated. - Evidence: agent event and tool-result path; event bus; optional reviewer example. - Basis: Bounded negative standard-distribution review. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Independent tests or reviewers implemented by a downstream application can change the result at that application&#39;s boundary; such a mode is not credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party future-oriented external-environment sensing → adaptation option → adoption into current operating capability circuit is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future shifts in models, tools or workloads might require adaptation, but the shipping runtime handles current conversations and lets an operator manage skills and templates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The SDK does not choose and adopt future capability alternatives from external intelligence; operators/developers install skills and update model/templates outside a closed agentic S4 loop. - Decision owner: Developer/operator for versions, providers and skill sets; per-task agent/tool choices remain S1. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hot skill scanning, SkillsManagementManager import/archive/export, model provider configuration, context compression, hooks and lifecycle events. - Closure path: Changed skill files may become visible on the next scan, but no controller has sensed a prospective environmental need, compared adaptation options and returned a chosen capability change to current work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Skill file CRUD and hot reload are mechanisms for carrying a user-selected change. Tool learning/context management during a present task is not a prospective organizational adaptation loop. - Evidence: skill runtime scan; separate skill-management service and explicit no-runtime scope; agent context compression. - Basis: Bounded negative with developer-managed future adaptation possibility distinguished from shipped S4. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An external agent using SDK tools to research future providers and update skills could create an S4 path in a larger system, but no such first-party feedback circuit is wired into this standard runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate identity/purpose-policy deliberation with legitimate returned binding governance is evidenced for the installed agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool safety and permission conflicts are handled as routine task-level policy constraints, not identity-level disputes between present execution and future adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A developer sets permission mode and allowed tools; an external user can approve/deny a pending tool call through agent.decide. Neither is a runtime ultimate-policy question with organizational deliberation and returned identity governance. - Decision owner: Developer/application operator for configured constraints and individual API approval; no qualified first-party S5 agent or parent identity authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionManager allow/deny/ask, mode handlers, system prompt, agent.decide, sandbox configuration, tool registration and agent template. - Closure path: Pending tool request → ordinary user/API permission decision → resume or deny the tool. This closes a single operational authorization, not a policy/identity issue with legitimate parent decision and return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model must obey runtime permission decisions, but these are preconfigured or call-by-call; a restrictive system prompt or human approval does not itself implement S5. - Evidence: permission evaluation; API permission resolution; template-based constraints. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking parent-governed and autonomous S5 paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The external hosting application&#39;s human owner may legitimately supply S5 in a broader organization, but that application&#39;s identity-policy escalation and return are not installed with the SDK.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/dc66c814c10a69e4f5d12ef33ae4c0be72bd400d/assessments/ko/kode-sdk.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit dc66c814c10a69e4f5d12ef33ae4c0be72bd400d\">dc66c81 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T15:34:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/moazbuilds/CodeMachine-CLI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CodeMachine CLI</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/moazbuilds/CodeMachine-CLI/tree/572def63eb808e95b18ccf6c69a13d7a13fe06fd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 572def63eb808e95b18ccf6c69a13d7a13fe06fd\">572def6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute discretionary coding/workflow instructions in a real workspace through configured model-backed coding agents and return their outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing codebase state and task intent, tool errors, provider availability, multi-step coding goals and responses from the workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The invoked coding-engine agent interprets the current task, chooses modifications/tool steps inside its own engine loop and responds to observations; CodeMachine&#39;s runtime launches and resumes that specific actor through a first-party installed command path. - Decision owner: Installed model-backed coding-work cells connected by CodeMachine runner; the cognition and inner tool loop are external-provider machinery, while first-party CodeMachine owns their task issuance, runtime integration and result/session return. Provider internals are not silently credited as first-party metasystems. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent templates, combined prompts, engine registry/auth cache/fallback, MCP tool context, abort/timeout, monitoring IDs, JSON event/status store, chained prompts, process stdout/session callbacks. - Closure path: CLI or TUI goal → first-party coordinator/step loader → executeAgent and external engine.run → model-backed tool/observation loop → output/status/session record → result or subsequent chained workflow step. - Boundary reachability: registerRunCommand calls CoordinatorService.execute; its executor directly calls executeAgent, which resolves and calls a real configured engine; the shipped workflow runner independently uses the same method. This is not only an abstract interface or test fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: If model-backed coding engines are removed, authentication checks, parser, prompt files, static workflow gates and logs remain, but no autonomous coding decisions or tool-mediated progress. Their internal intelligence is a dependency, yet the assembled first-party agent-operating mode is real. - Evidence: runnable CLI; execution groups; engine selection, run and result. - Basis: Structural deployed standard CLI/TUI path, assuming a supported coding CLI is configured. - Confidence: High for integration of the real agent executable; no source-independent performance assertion. - Caveats: Vendor model/tool loops and authorization are external; no live model execution was used in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party conflict-specific regulation between distinct S1 work cells is established for the standard local service recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel coding agents can edit overlapping workspace files or disagree about interfaces; the observed framework supports parallel/sequential work but no shipped collision detector, joint resource reservation or model negotiation response specific to such a disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user-written coordination DSL fixes parallel versus sequential execution; CoordinationExecutor starts and joins tasks, halts sequential groups on failure and reports status. These do not decide how to harmonize two conflicting agents or return corrective agreements to both. - Decision owner: Host-specified script/template order and deterministic group executor; possible project-specific coordination by spawned external coding agents is not a first-party shipped S2 relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CoordinatorParser, group mode, Promise.all, stop-on-error, inherited parent-agent IDs, monitor registry, workflow statuses and directive fields. - Closure path: A group&#39;s tasks yield results which advance or stop later groups. A concrete detected cross-agent interference and corresponding coordination outcome returned to modify both work cells is not shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: CoordinatorService naming, hierarchy, fan-out and stream ordering are scheduling/delegation. Potential file collisions in a shared workspace are not themselves evidence that CodeMachine ships a collision-attentuation path. - Evidence: scripted parallel/sequential groups; parent/child monitoring; workflow status/sequence. - Basis: Bounded negative under the Profile S2 inter-S1 disturbance and specific feedback test. - Confidence: Medium-high for standard shipped paths. - Caveats: A downstream project could implement collision-specific review/locks with custom agents and templates; the generic constructor alone does not qualify for C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No installed discretionary whole-current control over cross-agent resource commitments, priorities or performance at the CodeMachine service recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed coding agents, stalled sessions, excessive runtime and poor step output could warrant management; shipped records expose status and allow stop/resume, but the reviewed decisions remain fixed/session-local or human-preconfigured. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured scripts/templates choose step order; per-step directives may rewind a preset number of steps and a controller can converse with the user before execution. Neither source establishes a manager seeing all current operations and adaptively bargaining/reallocating their commitments as a whole. - Decision owner: Host/human for template/controller start, fixed directive/loop code for step retry/rewind, external engines for local coding choices. No first-party agent-owned S3 current-management mode or distinct operationally closed parent S3 loop is shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Monitoring DB/active agents, telemetry, abort/timeout, StatusService, step index manager, preset maxIterations, directive.json and human continue/checkpoint. - Closure path: A step may write an enabled loop directive, causing configured previous steps to re-execute; a checkpoint waits for human continue/quit. This lacks evidence that one current whole-system view justifies a managerial decision over resource/commitment allocation across the ongoing S1 population. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard loop and fail-stop controls change execution order but do not alone supply the S3-wide accountability/right; user pre-execution planning is goal setup, not the whole-now managerial loop. - Evidence: controller pre-execution conversation/continue; loop directive evaluation; rewind flow; runner status/engine fallback. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking strongest candidate (stepBack and controller). - Confidence: Medium: externally composed workflows can become strong management systems, but are not evidenced in this standard boundary. - Caveats: Loop/rewind is not ignored; it is an explicit first-party step-control mechanism, lacking a proven whole-current S3 decision-right witness and therefore not promoted merely by naming.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent alternative reading of S1 results and audit-finding return into S3 current control is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An external coding agent might falsely report success; logs, telemetry and retryable steps expose artifacts but remain normal self/runner reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Monitoring stores provider stdout, status and telemetry. Users may inspect logs, and custom workflow steps could review code, but no shipped complementary evaluator with independent raw evidence and correction-return authority is installed. - Decision owner: Runner/monitor records ordinary agent execution; optional operator review and downstream custom reviewer agents are outside a demonstrated built-in S3 channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Monitoring DB, log snapshots, session IDs, status graph, checkpoints and telemetry dashboards. - Closure path: Logs go to UI/CLI; failure statuses can halt steps. No independent sampled replay/ground-truth comparison is routed to a distinct S3 decision-right to correct first-party agent claims. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A transcript and a hypothetical review step are not themselves complementary operational evidence or independence. - Evidence: normal runner log/telemetry and status; coordinator results; human checkpoint. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high for public shipped operating modes. - Caveats: A project can embed an independent auditor in an agent template, but it must be evidenced on its own runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No recurring prospective external intelligence that develops adaptations and returns accepted changes into the harness&#39;s current operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tools, provider models, software requirements or architecture changes may demand future evolution; available workflow builder serves a user-requested task rather than a standing capability-governance cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator selects provider/model/imports and can ask Ali to design a workflow; no first-party S4 model chooses a capability migration based on ongoing environmental monitoring and returns it to live S3 operations. - Decision owner: Operator/configured downstream developer; currently invoked model-backed builder owns its S1 design output, not organizational S4 adaptation of CodeMachine as a whole. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent/config imports, Ali builder prompts, model fallback, template registries, runtime configuration and telemetry. - Closure path: A user can generate or select a new workflow; source does not show a standing outside-oriented sensing → evaluated adaptation options → incorporation into present operational control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A design/code-generation work product or a change of selected engine is not prospective organizational intelligence closure by default. - Evidence: shipped Ali builder configuration; imports and chosen template; engine fallback. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: User-constructed workflows may develop future options for their external organization; that is a separate system/recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate-identity dispute escalation, legitimate authority decision and binding return loop is installed in a CodeMachine service instance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model/tool permissions and workflows require consent, but those are ordinary operational safety envelopes, not observed identity-level policy conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User approves or pauses controller/workflow steps, chooses agent templates and model configuration; agents can request checkpoint/stop. No source route treats a foundational purpose/policy dispute as S5 and installs the resulting authoritative rule for subsequent work. - Decision owner: Human operator sets local permissions, goals and tools externally; model actors decide bounded coding tasks. No evidenced S5 ultimate-policy holder is part of an operational closure path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Interactive controller gate, checkpoint/quit, MCP filter context, engine auth, static config, permission-related vendor flags and directive constraints. - Closure path: Human continue transitions from planning to execution or lets a checkpoint pass. Neither is an identity-policy deliberation returned as durable binding governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A controller role, prompt instructions and user approval of an ordinary step cannot be promoted to S5 on names alone. - Evidence: controller continue gate; checkpoint confirmation; MCP context / auth envelope. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Downstream development organization might impose its own ultimate policy, but it is not automatically a closed CodeMachine product S5 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a22b4005f08acbf62e0bfc4f3e92fc4e49b79ef6/assessments/co/codemachine-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a22b4005f08acbf62e0bfc4f3e92fc4e49b79ef6\">a22b400 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T00:44:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/go-kratos/blades\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blades</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/go-kratos/blades/tree/b78fec59029d11e02c135ea10da8986ed006521f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b78fec59029d11e02c135ea10da8986ed006521f\">b78fec5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Handle current tasks through model-selected operations and tool execution, retaining actual observations for further model decisions and user output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended user prompts, multimodal inputs, changes in tool/API responses, errors, session context and subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Within the first-party agent.handle loop, the configured model selects tools/parameters or a final message based on prior history; tool execution returns actual observations into the next model request. - Decision owner: Configured model-directed Agent operating within Blades&#39; first-party loop. The remote model host is a provider dependency, while middleware/iteration limits and developer registrations constrain the agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: NewRunner, Session history, Invocation data, Tool handler, middleware, optional skill/tool resolution, maxIterations, message streaming and tool-exit signals. - Closure path: Input Runner.Run → agent Run/prepareInvocation → provider Generate or NewStreaming → RoleTool → executeTools/actual tool Handle result → session/local message history → next Generate or completed assistant answer. - Boundary reachability: NewAgent configures a concrete Agent.Run, not just an interface sketch; Runner actually calls it, and handle executes registered tools and re-enters provider decisions for both stateless and session-history modes. Optional NewDeepAgent returns an actual configured NewAgent with task/todo tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model decides next action from returned tool evidence; the deterministic tool handler, middleware and session are subordinate execution and data mechanisms. This is distinct from a purely prewritten graph sequence. - Evidence: agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; runner.go. - Basis: Explicit reachable source showing model/tool/observation/next-decision closure. - Confidence: High for first-party source structure; no external model/provider inference executed. - Caveats: Actual provider integration, tools and policy are supplied at deployment; task-level autonomy does not imply metasystem autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No verified first-party inter-S1 disturbance-specific damping relation with returned mutual adjustment is installed at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel subagent contention, inconsistent data writes and competing tool actions could occur; actual inter-unit conflict-response policy is not implemented in the reviewed flow primitives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ParallelAgent starts and streams peers; RoutingAgent selects a named recipient; LoopAgent enforces configured iteration/condition. None detects a specific organizational disturbance between independently viable S1 cells and authoritatively attenuates it. - Decision owner: Task model router, programmatic workflow composer and deterministic Go flow execution; no S2-specific mutual-coordination decision owner evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subagent clone/errgroup, cancellation, HandoffTool routing, loop exit, graph dependency scheduling, Session and nested task tool. - Closure path: Current task routing/parallel result/loop outputs reach the invoker; missing independent S1 conflict observation → S2-specific stabilization choice → enforced adjustments returned to affected distinct S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concurrent agents, cancellation and model-chosen handoff are useful orchestration, not automatically S2; no concrete disturbance-specific reciprocal dampening is established. - Evidence: flow/parallel.go; flow/routing.go; flow/loop.go; flow/deep.go; internal/deep/tasktool.go; graph/executor.go. - Basis: Bounded negative after explicit inspection of supported first-party flow/graph modes. - Confidence: Medium-high for source-inspected shipped paths. - Caveats: An adopter could supply a separate S2 algorithm via callbacks; hypothetical composition does not upgrade shipped framework.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No app-wide current-control system is evidenced that integrates several S1 operating units and makes binding resource/commitment/priority interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential multiple subagent workloads, resource exhaustion, deadlines and competing workflow commitments; actual aggregate management is not present in the inspected first-party decision path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: DeepAgent can model-select task delegation and update todos for its current task; Runner runs a root agent, and graph scheduler advances configured tasks. Neither represents a whole-current organizational command right across independent S1 purposes/commitments. - Decision owner: Task agent, developer-authored graph/flow configuration, deterministic Runner/graph executor. No S3 metasystem owner or legitimate parent return path proven. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskTool, WriteTodos, graph checkpoint/recovery, session tracking, max iterations, parallel cancellation and optional middleware. - Closure path: Current task → todo/delegation → feedback → task completion; absent whole-system current view → strategic present resource/priority/commitment adjudication → adjustment returned to distinct S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parent-subagent dispatch for one goal is S1 decomposition, not necessarily S3 management of an enduring organization; a checkpoint or todo list does not confer a S3 right. - Evidence: flow/deep.go; internal/deep/tasktool.go; internal/deep/writetodostool.go; runner.go; graph/executor.go; graph/executor.go. - Basis: Explicit current-work context/graph/delegation inspection; bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared application recursion. - Caveats: Complex third-party orchestration applications may themselves own S3; their business governance is outside this source-boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of operational reporting is connected by corrective feedback into normal current-control regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Inaccurate model outputs, weak artifacts or unmet criteria. A real Criteria LLM scorer exists for selected messages, but its returned Evaluation is not a shipped governance intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The optional model-backed scorer returns Pass, Score, Feedback to its caller; it does not independently interrogate ordinary multi-unit current control or own a returned corrective/withhold decision. - Decision owner: Optional scorer agent/model and adopter caller choosing scoring criteria/handling. No separately authorized S3 organizational corrective actor in the normal running app. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed evaluation JSON, model Run, optional diagnostics/test code, middleware confirmation and tool errors. - Closure path: Caller invokes evaluator with message → evaluator model outputs score/feedback → returned to caller; absent independently obtained conflicting operating evidence → S3 challenge → accountable correction/commitment revision returned into S1/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM judging is genuine model feedback, but optional endpoint called by an adopter is not by itself complementary audit with independent access and enforced correction. Model judge names or scoring output are insufficient. - Evidence: evaluator/criteria.go; evaluator/criteria.go; evaluator/criteria.go; evaluator/evaluator.go; flow/loop.go; middleware/confirm.go. - Basis: Source-backed evaluator input/output and separate workflow caller boundary, bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium; evaluator possibility explicitly inspected rather than dismissed. - Caveats: Specific external applications may wire evaluation to live corrective loops; outside declared first-party installed mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective environmental-intelligence and organizational capability-adaptation decision with accepted return into operating configuration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing longer-horizon integration/model/tool needs and shifting user tasks; session memory and skills may preserve context, but not select strategic organizational changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer configures model, tools, skills, recipe, memory and graph. Runtime can select operations, but no shipped autonomous future capability evaluation/approval/install right is established. - Decision owner: External developer/operator for configuration, task agent for current decisions, optional model evaluator for individual outputs; no operational S4 owner or legitimate parent-return future adaptation path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skill loader, memory and history, model adapters, configurable ToolResolver, graph checkpoint/replay and Recipe loader. - Closure path: Current request may read memory/skill instructions and operate within them; absent future environment sensing → alternative capability selection → authorized changed agent/tool topology → subsequent operations reflecting an S4 decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Pluggable design, tool registration, resumed graph and long-term memory are infrastructure for extensibility, not evidence of prospective system-level intelligence and closure. - Evidence: agent.go; agent.go; agent.go; session.go; skills/loader.go; memory/memory.go; recipe/loader.go. - Basis: Bounded negative of supported dynamic extension/memory/graph mechanisms. - Confidence: Medium-high for standard first-party modes. - Caveats: A customized third-party self-improving agent could use these tools as S4, but none is first-party coupled here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party final legitimate values/purpose/organizational identity decision and returned binding policy control path at the application recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool authorization, model/config identity and task-level confirmations, not proven ultimate organizational mission conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer defines system instructions, model and tool set; optional ConfirmFunc allows/denies individual invocation; no function settles legitimate ultimate ethos/purpose for this app. - Decision owner: Developer/operator who authored application constraints and callback, plus deterministic middleware that enforces tool/invocation admission; none a packaged S5 purpose-governing organ. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: System instruction precedence, ToolResolver, middleware Confirm, stop/exit, session model and custom provider config. - Closure path: Current invocation → approval callback → allowed/interrupted execution; missing policy-purpose dispute → authorized final organizational judgment → returned revised governing purpose for present operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A named agent, static instructions or per-tool human confirmation is not automatically ultimate authority over the system&#39;s identity and legitimate direction. - Evidence: agent.go; agent.go; middleware/confirm.go; middleware/confirm.go; runner.go. - Basis: Explicit source-control and middleware scope, bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high for shipped first-party API/modes. - Caveats: An actual adopter organization may supply S5 externally; this is not a packaged Blades owner and no parent-governed return is evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/473cdf60f60b630a52de16f2450c621f550fc59b/assessments/blades.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 473cdf60f60b630a52de16f2450c621f550fc59b\">473cdf6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T00:15:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lvndry/jazz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jazz</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lvndry/jazz/tree/957ad8ef522f9137f690c8acc319553f56e5bdb9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 957ad8ef522f9137f690c8acc319553f56e5bdb9\">957ad8e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a user/workflow objective into tool-mediated environmental outcomes through repeated model reasoning, tool selection, execution and result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task requirements, tool/environment state, provider responses, command/file/web results, approval outcomes, context pressure, failures and user/queued input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive response/tool action and revise subsequent behavior from returned tool/environment evidence. - Decision owner: the active Jazz model agent in the primary or recursive child run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentRunner, agent loop, tool executor/registry, conversation/context manager, approvals, retry/budget guards, memory, persistence and presentation surfaces. - Closure path: objective/context → model completion chooses tool/action → tool runtime executes/observes environment → tool result is appended to the run → next model completion observes changed evidence and chooses subsequent action → task/environment state changes. - Boundary reachability: standard first-party path used by terminal, headless, workflow/chat and recursive sub-agent execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime machinery bounds and executes choices, but removing the model removes the substantive next-action judgment while leaving only transport/enforcement mechanisms. - Evidence: agent-loop.ts; agent-runner.ts; subagent-tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination relation is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no complete qualifying inter-S1 interference/oscillation witness is established among Jazz operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S2 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic tool concurrency cap, recursive child depth/iteration bounds, background shell-job queue, workflow scheduler, peer messaging and approval serialization constrain execution but do not by themselves establish S2. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed standard path reconstructs distinct S1 agents → concrete inter-S1 disturbance → S2-specific attenuation → feedback changing subsequent S1 behavior. - Distinct S1 units: a parent can spawn one or more recursive child agents with their own fresh conversations and complete model/tool loops. - Inter-S1 disturbance: not established as a concrete organizational interaction. Generic contention for MAXCONCURRENTTOOLS is tool-runtime capacity and no child-specific disturbance is surfaced into child behavior; shell jobs are not autonomous S1s. - Attenuating coordination relation: generic tool scheduling, recursion depth bounds and job-worker concurrency prevent resource exhaustion but are not shown regulating a concrete child-to-child operational disturbance. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child final results return to the parent, but no coordination verdict about an inter-S1 disturbance is returned to affected child S1s or otherwise closes an S2 relation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is deliberately not mapped as S2. spawnsubagent, askpeer, schedules and job fan-out are delegation/communication/execution primitives without the required inter-S1 disturbance-and-feedback witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no material S2 function is established, so ownership classification does not proceed. - Evidence: subagent-tools.ts; tool-executor.ts; job-queue-service.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system inside-and-now current-control loop is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the parent agent decomposes its own task and the daemon/scheduler regulates execution mechanics, but no whole-deployment current operational variety is managed through a distinct S3 current view/decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established over current commitments/resources/priorities of all credited S1 units. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnsubagent, returned child results, workflow scheduler, job-batch status/cancel, approvals, daemon retries/recovery and normal run state. - Closure path: not applicable; delegation supplies a task and later a child result, but the standard path does not close whole-system current view → S3 judgment → current intervention across S1 commitments → renewed whole-system feedback. - Boundary reachability: the controls are first-party and reachable, but reachability/delegation does not establish the S3 function. - Whole-system current view: not established. spawnsubagent is a synchronous tool-style child run with a final result; no standard live child-tree list/status surface was established for the parent. - Current-control decision scope: the model can decide whether to spawn another bounded child, but no first-party path was found for persistent whole-tree steering, reassignment or interruption based on a current aggregate view. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no qualifying S3 function is established. Generic model orchestration of delegated subtasks remains part of current S1 task execution rather than a separately evidenced metasystemic regulator. - Evidence: subagent-tools.ts; scheduler-service.ts; job-worker.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary-audit closure is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Jazz exposes transcripts, run history, telemetry, eval/development checks and can run review tasks, but no materially independent audit loop is established over ordinary runtime claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: logs/run history/telemetry, repository evals, GitHub PR-review workflow capability, generic recursive sub-agents and approval gates. - Closure path: not applicable; reviewed surfaces can observe or evaluate work but no standard runtime path closes complementary access → independent audit judgment → finding returned into current organizational control. - Claim being audited: no function-specific first-party claim/loop established for audit of the assessed Jazz organization. - Ordinary reporting path: normal model/tool transcript, tool results, metrics and workflow/job status. - Complementary access path: evals/review tasks can inspect outputs or repository state, but they are task/development surfaces rather than a distinct production audit channel over Jazz operations. - Independence boundary: no audit-specific actor/sensor boundary separate from ordinary runtime reporting was established. - Who acts on findings: a user or parent agent can act on a review result, but that generic composition is not a shipped S3 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no S3 function is established before ownership classification. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/agent/tools/subagent-tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then organizational adaptation loop is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web/search/peer information and memory can influence current/future task responses, but no organizationally scoped external/prospective adaptation function is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/search tools, askpeer, scheduled workflows, durable memory/context extraction, plugins/MCP and model/provider configuration. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed path reconstructs external/future-relevant sensing → explicit adaptation option development → adaptation judgment → return into present Jazz organizational capability/control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Jazz agents can autonomously research and remember information, but generic research/learning is part of task execution unless it participates in a demonstrated external-and-prospective organizational adaptation conversation. - Evidence: peer-tools.ts; packages/core/src/agent/context/memory-extractor.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persona, system prompt, permissions, approval policy, tool grants and user rules constrain operation but are not shown as identity-level policy issues resolved by an ultimate authority loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S5 within the standard Jazz deployment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent JSON configuration, persona/system instructions, permissions/approvals, auto-approval policy, peer grants, tool allowlists and operator configuration. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed first-party path shows an identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching legitimate ultimate authority and returning as authoritative policy governing subsequent Jazz operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents operate within configured identity/permission boundaries; they are not shown exercising ultimate authority over those boundaries. Operator approval/configuration alone is not reclassified as S5. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/agent/tools/subagent-tools.ts; packages/core/src/agent/tools/peer-tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: Jazz intentionally makes agent rules and permissions configurable; Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat configuration existence or ordinary approval as S5 without identity/ultimate-policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4a9a3df427159997173949c13597f68b727f2309/assessments/jazz.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4a9a3df427159997173949c13597f68b727f2309\">4a9a3df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:18:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/THUDM/AgentRL\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentRL</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/THUDM/AgentRL/tree/6a73409d31ba695d383b978a8ad3ef400d90c054\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6a73409d31ba695d383b978a8ad3ef400d90c054\">6a73409 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: each rollout agent can execute a multiturn environment task autonomously and produce operational trajectories.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: Ray placement, task queues and worker pools coordinate training infrastructure, not interference among autonomous operational S1 units pursuing shared live commitments. The shallow S2=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: Task Manager/trainer scheduling governs the training pipeline rather than current operational commitments of a viable multi-S1 system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: reward/loss computation evaluates training trajectories for optimization, not an independent complementary audit channel governing live S1 work.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: RL converts historical environment outcomes into updated model parameters, but it is an offline/development training loop rather than a distinct runtime S4 function scanning future external conditions and adapting organizational capability. The shallow S4=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: reward design, tasks, training configuration and deployment purpose remain externally supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c6f4922f94d64503565f75530e237dac6540e155/assessments/agentrl.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c6f4922f94d64503565f75530e237dac6540e155\">c6f4922 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:31:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Kocoro-lab/Shannon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shannon</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Kocoro-lab/Shannon/tree/391b619130281502d227a53dd97238c9791bf174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 391b619130281502d227a53dd97238c9791bf174\">391b619 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. AgentLoops perform bounded model/tool work and adapt subsequent actions from observations. The agent actor owns the next operational action within configured limits.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. Swarm Lead decomposition, delegation, result collection and convergence do not establish a separate first-party mechanism regulating a concrete inter-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation. The historical A promoted orchestration into S2 without the required disturbance/regulation witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: C. Budget management, preflight checks and backpressure regulate shared current execution capacity across the running system. The runtime supplies and enforces this whole-system resource-control path; applications configure its bounds.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. No materially different access path to operational reality was established. Validation, sandbox and policy checks remain controls on ordinary execution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. No first-party outside-and-then loop converts external/future environmental information into organizational adaptation and returns it to current capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Durable human approval gates ordinary risky/current actions. It does not establish a function-specific identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching legitimate S5 authority and returning as organizational policy. OPA/configuration surfaces likewise do not themselves close S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4/assessments/shannon.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4\">cfe2730 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-22T20:38:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vinhnx/VTCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">VTCode</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vinhnx/VTCode/tree/dc32271158bec3c37b81ad7bf57157a801307517\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dc32271158bec3c37b81ad7bf57157a801307517\">dc32271 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out user-scoped coding tasks through model-selected terminal/file/patch/search tools and iterative observation; optionally carry out bounded delegated subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Varying user requirements and source structures, build/test failures, provider responses, filesystem and terminal output, and findings during code changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The active model chooses task-specific tools/actions and subsequent continuation from returned tool outputs. First-party child agents have their own model-guided runner and tools within configured permissions. - Decision owner: VTCode AgentRunner model response and active child model sessions; typed tool registry executes selected actions under platform/user restrictions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model/provider adapter, tool dispatcher, sandbox and command policy, task tracker, session memory, auto-compaction, verification/continuation controls, child lifecycle statuses and bounded budgets. - Closure path: User task or delegated prompt → provider tool decision → executable tool action → result in session context → next model choice or completion/verification; child findings may return to parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reasoning over current source/output and choosing concrete actions is task-owned autonomy, even though permission policy, operator plan approval and provider contracts remain external constraints. - Boundary reachability: vtcode interactive and vtcode exec load this agent/tool runtime, and the registered subagent facade can construct first-party child AgentRunner sessions with effective workspace and permissions. - Evidence: CLI/agent flow, main task model/tool feedback loop, batched tool dispatch, child model-runner configuration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for first-party operational wiring; no empirical model-backed successful coding task is claimed. - Caveats: Child capabilities vary by spec, model and tool policies; the default explorer is intentionally read-only.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Detect and attenuate real interference between independent S1 coding cells, then return a compensating coordination decision into ongoing units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential concurrent child edits to the same project files, competing branch changes, conflicting integration results and resource collisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The controller spawns/waits and may create optional isolated worktrees. The completion hook&#39;s heuristic diff/merge is not a demonstrated returned inter-S1 adjustment: built-in child specs default to no worktree, the branch/worktree name handed to reconciliation differs from creation, and outcomes are logged rather than propagated as child/parent coordination decisions. - Decision owner: Fixed SubagentController lifecycle/worktree utilities and parent task delegator; no source-backed separate owner closing actual cross-S1 conflict detection/attenuation/return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child concurrency/depth caps, optional WorktreeManager, git worktree locks, read-only explorer permission, status/wait/close, heuristic unsafe-added-line check and git merge failure handling. - Closure path: Parent delegate → children perform bounded tasks → statuses/results returned; optional child worktree → completion reconciliation attempt. A conflict-aware correction received by independently operating affected children is not evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parallel worktrees can prevent direct write collisions and git may reject merges, but source does not show a working regulated inter-unit feedback relationship rather than isolation, delegation and a post-hoc integration attempt. - Evidence: built-in worker/explorer isolation none, generated worktree name on spawn, after-child reconciliation with spec.name, reconciler hard-codes main branch and logs result, git verify and merge. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; concurrency/worktree mechanisms are real but integration effectiveness is untested and a naming mismatch is visible in source. - Caveats: A repaired or externally coordinated worktree installation could produce new evidence for S2. This is not an assertion of any reproduced runtime merge failure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Make binding current whole-system resource, priority and commitment decisions over distinct viable S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple subagent requests, shared task progress, accumulated spend/time, incomplete subtasks and accepted/rejected local work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The parent can delegate, wait, cancel and integrate findings, while plan/build/evaluate controls one current task. No distinct whole-current portfolio tradeoff owner reassigns resources/commitments across operating cells on their shared current state. - Decision owner: Parent task agent for delegation and fixed controller for caps; no additional evidenced S3-specific manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subagent status/notifications, max depth and concurrency, task tracker, runtime budget, session handoff and single-threaded spec/contract/evaluator revisions. - Closure path: Subtask prompt → child status/report → parent coding turn; plan spec → generator → task evaluator → possible retry. This is work delivery/QA inside S1, not governance of all current interdependent operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parent retains local task control, but performing decomposition does not prove whole-current current-control authority with accountable cross-unit commitments. - Evidence: subagent queue and concurrency, wait/result flow, single-threaded PBE boundary, task-local planner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded assessment. - Caveats: Enterprise scheduling external to one session is a different recursion, not silently included here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Complement current management claims with sufficiently independent operational evidence and return actionable correction to S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect patches, false task completion, missed acceptance criteria, unsafe diffs and overconfident agent output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verifyproposedchange can spawn a read-only verifier, and opt-in PBE uses a skeptical evaluator or a multi-model skeptic panel; they judge the local change/spec/verification, not the whole-current S3 operational account. Worktree reconciliation instead calls a deterministic line heuristic. - Decision owner: Task-level reviewer model/evaluator and heuristic diff gate; no S3 management-audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only explorer/verifier, explicit PASS/REJECT parsing, evaluator scorecard and high-severity findings, task tracker contracts, model skeptic panel, git changed-file summaries. - Closure path: Code changes and task spec → reviewer/evaluator findings → local S1 revision or user report. This is artifact/task QA; an S3 state or claim is not independently observed and corrected. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Separate models may challenge local work genuinely, but independence of a task reviewer is not the independence/authority of a complementary managerial audit channel. - Evidence: verifier agent and fail-closed verdict, worktree heuristic instead of model reviewer, evaluator and skeptic option, PBE single-threaded architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A QA tool can materially improve reliability without crossing the VSM S3 boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Model external future changes/opportunities and choose organizational capability adaptations with return to current management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing developer needs, model/tool ecosystem, future projects and software/repository practices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Plan/build/evaluate can ask a model to replan the current rejected implementation and record bounded, task-scoped generalization notes. Skills, context and scheduling may alter tasks, but no installed separate prospective environmental intelligence and S3-mediated capability choice is evidenced. - Decision owner: Local task planner/replanner or human config/skill author; no demonstrated S4 strategist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task spec/contract, acceptance criteria, scorecard, task generalization notes with falsifiers, local session memory, external MCP/skills and opt-in durable vtcode schedule invocations. - Closure path: Current implementation → skeptical evaluation → revised task plan → continued main execution; no future external environment → alternative capability strategy → S3 handoff and adoption closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Genuine model re-planning is confined to present-task correctness; available external tools and task-triggered schedules do not prove autonomous anticipation or organizational adaptation. - Evidence: task-local reevaluation and replan, evidence-bounded generalization and evaluator prompt, runtime architecture and scheduling, loop engineering workflow scope. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within shipping first-party paths. - Caveats: Users may explicitly ask VTCode to research or implement future tooling; the general S1 capability to accept such prompts is not an autonomous first-party S4 mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimately settle ultimate organizational purpose, identity and governing values, including tensions between current operation and proposed future strategy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Untrusted workspace instructions, malicious commands, protected files, conflicting tool privileges and unsafe/unapproved implementation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator/user decides task and approves plans/workspaces; fixed tool policy and sandbox control allowable actions. The runtime has no legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision right beyond applying these choices. - Decision owner: User and application-defined command/workspace safety gates; not a first-party autonomous S5 holder. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planning review gate, workspace trust, ToolPolicyGateway, command policy, sandbox, AGENTS.md context, permission profiles, tool denial paths and human consent. - Closure path: User task/policy → tool catalog and dispatch approval → accepted/denied S1 action; plan finalization explicitly awaits user approval before build. No new binding agent-level ultimate policy is decided and returned to an S3/S4 balance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enforcing preconfigured policy and producing a proposed plan is operational accountability, not legitimate authority to define VTCode&#39;s ultimate purpose or override the user. - Evidence: planning human handoff and read-only enforcement, security/model and command policy, configurable approval/sandbox components, project instructions as non-security context. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high on human and fixed-rule gate boundary; medium-high on absent S5. - Caveats: Code safety, review and honest handoffs are useful but not proof of S5 self-governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d6f173cbcb069780266193e1b2597ee7fef11a9d/assessments/vt/vtcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d6f173cbcb069780266193e1b2597ee7fef11a9d\">d6f173c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:41:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TencentCloudADP/youtu-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Youtu-Agent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TencentCloudADP/youtu-agent/tree/c2caa539f4c95ae1c39ed24dc8a99cb3651e1d5d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c2caa539f4c95ae1c39ed24dc8a99cb3651e1d5d\">c2caa53 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: simple and worker agents autonomously choose tools/actions for bounded tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the planner statically decomposes and sequences worker tasks; workers do not mutually regulate shared constraints or interference. Generated workflow primitives likewise require application-defined coordination. The shallow S2=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the planner owns a current task plan and worker selection, but lacks the persistent whole-system progress/resource/commitment regulation, feedback-based reassignment or replanning required for S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no separate independent complementary audit channel over operational work was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: agent generation and training/practice can improve later behavior, but they are development/training mechanisms rather than a runtime prospective environmental-intelligence function governing adaptation. The shallow S4=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: generated prompts/configuration, worker roster and overall mission/policy remain externally framed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a/assessments/youtu-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a\">2d58729 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:41:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QuantaAlpha/RepoMaster\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RepoMaster</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QuantaAlpha/RepoMaster/tree/9cf59930102970d61d6eb2f702fd936d3cda7f11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9cf59930102970d61d6eb2f702fd936d3cda7f11\">9cf5993 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: repository/code agents autonomously explore, execute and react to task results inside their configured mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: scheduler→tool/agent routing and sequential fallback are delegation/orchestration. No mechanism was established for regulating interference, oscillation or shared constraints among multiple autonomous S1 units. The shallow S2=C interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the scheduler manages a current problem-solving plan but does not expose whole-system authority over persistent shared resources, capacities or multi-S1 commitments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: result evaluation is performed by the same scheduler trajectory; no separate complementary audit channel was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: web/repository search gathers external information for the current objective, not a prospective intelligence function responsible for adapting future system strategy/capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: modes, prompts, tools and termination policy are developer-defined.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a/assessments/repomaster.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a\">2d58729 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:41:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pi</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/tree/71dca871bc80b6bc97be37f0ca3189399d651fff\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 71dca871bc80b6bc97be37f0ca3189399d651fff\">71dca87 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: autonomous model/tool iteration is the core first-party abstraction.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: message queues and steering coordinate turns within one agent session; no mechanism was found for regulating interference among multiple autonomous S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: runtime callbacks and session controls do not create whole-system current authority over a multi-S1 organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no independent/complementary audit path distinct from normal tool-loop execution was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: context preparation, compaction and model changes between turns support current execution rather than a distinct prospective environment-intelligence function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: system prompts, tools, providers and stop/steering policy are parent/application supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a/assessments/pi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d5872972b06cc594960efefa39b785e6b88271a\">2d58729 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T04:01:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MiroMindAI/MiroThinker\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MiroThinker</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MiroMindAI/MiroThinker/tree/1c4253f6774bf40314271a827304b842100e054c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1c4253f6774bf40314271a827304b842100e054c\">1c4253f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: research agents autonomously choose search/tool actions and iterate from evidence/results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: subagents are invoked as operational capabilities and return results to the main agent. Duplicate-query and rollback protections operate inside individual agent loops, not between autonomous S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: orchestration of current turns/subtasks does not establish whole-system regulation of shared resources, capacities or commitments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: rollback/format checking is ordinary execution control, not a distinct complementary audit channel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: broad external research is performed to answer the current task; post-training/trajectory refinement is not a runtime prospective intelligence function governing future organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: models, prompts, tools and organizational topology remain externally configured.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c62c112b9468bc60e2a63b92623b0b69fd6248a6/assessments/mirothinker.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c62c112b9468bc60e2a63b92623b0b69fd6248a6\">c62c112 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T18:58:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TurixAI/TuriX-CUA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TuriX-CUA</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TurixAI/TuriX-CUA/tree/88c777bddfec9796667445873886a3563aa27246\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 88c777bddfec9796667445873886a3563aa27246\">88c777b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: First-party model-directed GUI task execution with observation/action/result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing macOS screens, UI element locations, login prompts, incomplete subtasks, unexpected app responses and errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Brain LLM interprets screenshots and chooses a current procedural goal; Actor LLM chooses corresponding GUI actions and arguments; first-party agent loop decides when to continue/stop on current outcome. - Decision owner: One first-party Agent system implementing model-driven Brain/Actor decision turns; inference providers supply models, not an additional prebuilt external computer-use harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MacUITreeBuilder screenshot/accessibility capture, Controller action registry, structured output parser, task history, action results, failure/step limits, optional Planner/skills/search/memory. - Closure path: GUI screenshot/previous action → Brain nextgoal and analysis → Actor action JSON → installed Controller.multiact executes on macOS → next screenshot/action history → Brain reevaluates and selects next goal. - Boundary reachability: examples/main.py instantiates shipped Agent with supplied Brain/Actor/Memory and optional Planner models, and calls Agent.run(maxsteps); run loops brainstep and actorstep, which invokes first-party Controller.multiact. No separate tool-providing external agent is required for this code path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Models make concrete runtime choice of next goal and GUI actions; execution is not limited to static hard-coded macros. The orchestrator/Controller still enforce data/step/action bounds. - Evidence: examples/main.py; src/agent/service.py; src/agent/views.py; src/controller/service.py; src/mac/actions.py. - Basis: Source-level reachable model GUI observation/feedback loop. - Confidence: High for source reachability; no external macOS/live-model execution attempted. - Caveats: Requires macOS native frameworks and a working configured provider; real task performance not established.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party mutual attenuation of interference among distinct independently operating S1 units proven. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A model Brain/Actor disagreement concerns one task&#39;s sequence and action quality, not a documented conflict among separate concurrent S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No coordinator detecting and attenuating actual contention across separate worker tasks, desktop agents or resource reservations. - Decision owner: One Agent&#39;s sequential execution scheduler, which passes single-task state between subroles. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Structured role outputs, action list, one-step loop, last-step history, optional plan and memory. - Closure path: Result flows between Brain and Actor for the same task; no separate inter-S1 disturbance → coordination choice → changed sibling activity feedback path evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Brain, Actor, Memory and Planner are model calls in a single operation. Distinct LLM configurations do not create distinct organizational S1 operations. - Evidence: src/agent/service.py; src/agent/plannerservice.py; src/agent/messagemanager/service.py; examples/main.py. - Basis: Bounded negative structural search at frozen default distribution. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An external multi-agent system may run multiple instances but its coordination is not supplied by this pinned core.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned current whole-system commitments/resources regulation beyond the one task&#39;s own planner/controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current GUI subgoal failure, incomplete plan steps, retries and model output errors within one operational task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Brain chooses current next goal and optional Planner composes current user&#39;s task; no actor chooses among organization-wide competing active tasks, priorities, resources or responsibilities. - Decision owner: One task-level Brain/Actor agent; user supplies task, and deterministic loop enforces max steps/failure cap. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Current screenshot evaluation, Planner plan generation, pause/stop controls, max failures/steps. - Closure path: Next goal → individual task action → next task observation, not separate whole-operational control → reallocation/return across concurrent operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Brain is a task planner/evaluator, not whole-system S3 despite &#39;planner&#39; labels, and the task&#39;s bounded loop is not a delegated operational-management position. - Evidence: src/agent/service.py; src/agent/plannerservice.py; examples/config.json. - Basis: Bounded negative review of decisive assignment and resource authority. - Confidence: Medium-high within declared single-run operational recursion. - Caveats: Additional multi-agent orchestration in an external OpenClaw runtime is not credited to TuriX-CUA.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary access/challenge of operational claims plus corrective return separate from ordinary execution was evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Action success/failure and changed UI state are ordinary primary task feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The same Brain that determines next task decisions also judges previous-step screenshot success; Controller returns local action results, not an independently owned auditor verdict. - Decision owner: Within-task Brain/model and deterministic controller/result parser. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Screenshot comparisons, stepevaluate, saved action histories, action errors and in-task memory summaries. - Closure path: Actor executes → Brain sees next screenshot and self-checks → Brain picks task continuation. This is first-party S1 feedback, not an alternative independent auditor collecting separately scoped operational evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prompt-separated Brain versus Actor gives functional division but same run/controller authority. A different model role alone is not enough to demonstrate independent audit rights. - Evidence: src/agent/service.py; src/agent/prompts.py; src/agent/views.py; src/controller/service.py. - Basis: Bounded negative ownership/independence comparison. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user can manually inspect a GUI, but no shipped agent-independent audit return is evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No supported external prospective change-of-organizational-capability option and return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unseen webpage changes and task uncertainty are current operational disturbances; model/GUI capabilities are specified through configured endpoints/skills. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Optional Planner may search or choose a skill for the present user task; Memory compresses/retrieves prior action context. Neither chooses/testing a new future capability for the harness and returns it to subsequent operation. - Decision owner: Task-driven model roles and operator-managed code/skill/model settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PlannerSearch and skill selection, Memory LLM summaries, record persistence and resumed action history. - Closure path: Search/record → better present task steps; no current-capability evidence → prospective alternatives → adaptation selection/application → later organizational operation closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Learning a webpage or summarizing an execution trace is not future adaptation of the computer-use organization&#39;s capability. - Evidence: src/agent/plannerservice.py; src/agent/service.py; src/utils/skills.py; src/utils/recordstore.py. - Basis: Bounded review of optional planning/search/memory and configured skills. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The operator could install new skills manually outside the runtime; not evidence of an installed S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy decision rights or returned resolution for the computer-use system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Human login/preferences, step-stop limits, model instructions and selected provider/tool capabilities are task-level constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator chooses starting task and may cancel; Brain askhuman asks for clarification. No supported actor decides system-wide ultimate ethos/identity conflict and returns binding policy. - Decision owner: Human task originator and externally maintained software settings; no function-specific S5 owner evidenced in the shipped harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: User task prompt, askhuman, pause/stop and force-stop hotkey, configured model API and skills. - Closure path: User clarifies an input or cancels a task; there is no independent policy controversy → ultimate authority decision → policy return to organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static prompts/user approval do not equal agent-owned or parent-governed S5 under the active Methodology. - Evidence: src/agent/views.py; src/agent/service.py; examples/main.py; examples/config.json. - Basis: Bounded negative source review of human and task authority. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A deploying organization may have its own user approval policy, outside the credited system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/093016a38ca74a0929f3e6eb9d6ac5a7a62fa831/assessments/turix-cua.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 093016a38ca74a0929f3e6eb9d6ac5a7a62fa831\">093016a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T17:11:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Nano-Collective/nanocoder\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nanocoder</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Nano-Collective/nanocoder/tree/258a916ee5bff51564635100a71625b63fb99673\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 258a916ee5bff51564635100a71625b63fb99673\">258a916 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: turn a natural-language or subscribed skill task into repository inspection, model-selected tool actions and a reported outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository contents, unfamiliar files, tool failures, user objectives, provider responses and triggered skill inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main agent or a delegated/triggered subagent selects next actions after model/tool feedback. - Decision owner: the first-party model-backed main or subagent instance; the daemon&#39;s event matcher merely schedules the latter. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, context/message store, permission resolver, mode-specific tool filtering, prompt builder, configurable model providers and per-project daemon. - Closure path: prompt or event → agent model/tool choice → tool execution and result → agent model&#39;s next choice or final outcome. - Boundary reachability: the supported terminal/headless runner and agent tool construct the shipped executors; the optional daemon wires subscription dispatch into a real subagent executor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent selects operations based on changing feedback, unlike the fixed event router or tool executor. - Evidence: conversation-loop.tsx, agent-tool.tsx, subagent-executor.ts. - Basis: source-verified, with first-party fake-provider/script tests; no live LLM-agent performance run. - Confidence: high for the shipped execution path, not measured task quality. - Caveats: no default guarantee that a particular optional skill or subagent is configured, and subscribed runs require the daemon to be running.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate destructive overlap and event storms among separate model-backed runs of one daemon subscription. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a burst of file changes or another cron/file event can start multiple concurrent project-mutating agents for the same skill before a previous run completes. - Distinct S1 units: potential overlapping SubagentExecutor run instances from one registered agent:/command: subscription; each is a separately deciding model agent, not a mere tool call. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate concurrent executions of one skill could race over the same work surface and overload local resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: fixed first-party per-subscription admission/suppression of overlapping triggered runs, not the model&#39;s choice of downstream task work. - Attenuating coordination relation: the subscription ID identifies the shared project task cell; a deterministic in-flight exclusion gate prevents two such independently deciding cells from running at the same time, and the file watcher coalesces repeated changes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: permitted event delivery starts the model worker; suppressed concurrent events do not start competing workers, while subsequent eligible events can again launch one after the previous run exits. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the code enforces an exclusion precisely against duplicate concurrently mutating agent runs for the same subscription, not merely moving messages or assigning children; unlike a global mutex it is narrowly scoped to this inter-run disturbance. - Coordination channel and decisive right: BackpressureDispatcher checks a subscription-specific in-flight set and deterministically drops a new event if a run with that ID is active; file changes are trailing-edge debounced. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic runtime C, not the triggered agent or an autonomous S2 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EventRouter registered subscription identity, BackpressureDispatcher in-flight set/debouncer and SkillDispatcher model-backed execution. - Closure path: matching event → per-subscription gate → at most one in-flight model run for that subscription; excess events are dropped and later eligible events can execute. - Boundary reachability: the startDaemon production path constructs the router → backpressure wrapper → skill dispatcher → model-backed executor and starts actual filesystem/cron event sources when the operator enables daemon mode. - Boundary reachability: startDaemon wraps the actual skill dispatcher with BackpressureDispatcher before subscribing file watcher/cron sources; script test explicitly checks drop-while-in-flight. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: protective inter-run scheduling is fixed source logic; the agents do not choose or negotiate interference responses. - Evidence: daemon.ts, backpressure.ts, backpressure.spec.ts. - Basis: explicit code wiring and deterministic first-party script test. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded daemon overlap case, low for general multi-agent collaboration. - Caveats: not a global project lock; different subscriptions or ordinary parallel agent calls may still edit the same files concurrently. Excess triggers are dropped, not durably queued or reconciled.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated whole-current operational management with selectable priorities/resources and an intervention return across the assessed agent cohort. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing active commitments, slow/stuck workers or cross-subscription project resource conflicts could need higher-level decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main model can delegate tasks and consume each worker&#39;s final result; a human can choose the overall mode or interrupt a turn; the daemon enforces a fixed per-subscription cap. No model/operator current-work portfolio decision with a sufficiently specific management return is demonstrated. - Decision owner: none established for whole-cohort S3; model and human retain local task/delegation/permission rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel subagent progress map and UI, depth/fan-out limits, abort signal, mode resolver, task list, daemon per-subscription gate and event logging. - Closure path: subagent final result → originating parent&#39;s ordinary next task decision, or top-level cancel; no current cohort inspection → selective commitment/resource intervention → workers&#39; ongoing changed operations path was evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: delegation, UI progress and bounded concurrency alone do not prove autonomous or parent-governed S3 resource management. - Evidence: tool-executor.tsx, subagent-events.ts, subagent-executor.ts. - Basis: structural source inspection and bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: external organizations could wrap Nanocoder with higher-level governance, which is not first-party S3 in this local product boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently accessed check of S1 operational claims with a finding fed to a responsible S3 controller was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false claims of work completion and uncontrolled code changes may occur, but routine tool outputs or test execution are not separate audit ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the producer model can run tests or use hooks; a user may review actions, and custom reviewer agents may be authored, but no independent first-party complementary audit is mandated/wired with corrective closure. - Decision owner: none established for an S3 audit function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool hooks, verification helpers, checkpoints, code diagnostics, subagent isolation, logs and first-party AVA tests. - Closure path: ordinary tool and hook results → same acting agent or user approval; no complementary audit claim → S3 corrective return is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: optional user-defined reviewers and adjacent tests cannot be counted as first-party production audit merely because they can inspect code. - Evidence: subagent-executor.ts, approval-policy.ts, subagents.md. - Basis: explicit design and bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a downstream team can construct a genuine independent review skill, but an available construction capability is not a demonstrated shipped audit circuit.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed future-facing external-environment option development and return to harness capability was identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing model capabilities, users, ecosystem constraints and future task demands would require strategic adaptation, whereas file/cron events are present-state triggers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the working model may consult external tools, maintain memory or revise a task plan; a configured skill may react to file changes. None is demonstrated as first-party prospective adaptation governance. - Decision owner: current S1 task agent for immediate choices and external human/maintainers for later product or skill redesign. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: semantic memory, web/MCP tools, plan/architect modes, skill manifests, cron/file subscriptions and checkpoints. - Closure path: current observations → local task response, or event → registered task run; no future environmental model → adaptation options → capability-update/control dialogue path is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reactive subscriptions and a generic skill constructor do not themselves create S4; no default research-to-redesign loop is shipped. - Evidence: skills.md, dispatcher.ts, daemon.ts. - Basis: bounded first-party path review. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: external users could implement a prospective watcher, but that is a configured use case, not credited as default harness S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no in-boundary ultimate-identity/policy decision and authoritative closure is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risky command execution and permitted tools are constrained locally; higher-level conflicts over organizational ethos or future-versus-present policy remain external. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the user selects modes, preauthorizes tools or answers operational prompts; skill authors configure permissions. This is not an identity-level adjudication with S3–S4 return. - Decision owner: no first-party legitimate S5 actor or closed parent arrangement established within the operating product. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: resolveToolApproval, alwaysAllow, mode-sensitive tool availability, blocked subagent tools and headless/plan constraints. - Closure path: per-tool approval/mode configuration governs individual operations; no policy-identity proposal → legitimate ultimate authority → system-wide decision return path is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static developer rules and granular user consent are action permission, not VSM S5 decision rights. - Evidence: approval-policy.ts, development-modes.md, subagent-executor.ts. - Basis: explicit first-party permission mechanisms and bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a separately governed organization can set an identity for deployments; this assessment does not equate the product&#39;s maintainers with a live S5 parent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/57ad4643834168eed26c27056e2d4e04cdc0ce4b/assessments/na/nanocoder.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 57ad4643834168eed26c27056e2d4e04cdc0ce4b\">57ad464 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T00:14:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openonion/connectonion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ConnectOnion</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openonion/connectonion/tree/7011faa911e2563e485f865db5be60f6e595f1bd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7011faa911e2563e485f865db5be60f6e595f1bd\">7011faa &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform user-specified work by model-directed choice of first-party registered tools/subagents and respond using returned execution observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing freeform user requests, tool failures/outputs, subagent responses, environment state, interruptions and context changes during the turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent model chooses next tool call or final response given accumulated messages/tool observations; a delegated subagent makes local model decisions within its allowed tools. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed Agent/concrete co ai first-party execution loop; provider infrastructure is a dependency, not an imported organizational actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LLM adapters, ToolRegistry and tool executor, conversation/trace, explicit operator approvals, TodoList, optional subagent and skill registration, iteration bounds and stop gates. - Closure path: User/input or hosted inbound prompt → Agent.input → runiterationloop → getllmdecision → first-party tool executor or delegated task → captured toolresult/error in messages/trace → next model decision/answer or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: The exported Agent.input directly calls the real run loop; default createagent registers first-party plugins and tools on that concrete Agent, not only a sample. Subagent tool explicitly instantiates and executes a real local Agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-selected tool/response decisions condition on live observations; executor, provider transport, static permissions and scheduled entry do not substitute for the agent&#39;s choices. - Evidence: agent.py; agent.py; agent.py; toolexecutor.py; subagents.py; agent.py. - Basis: Explicit first-party runtime source and actual tool/result return. - Confidence: High for shipped code structure/reachability; no live provider workload benchmark in this review. - Caveats: Requires configured provider, runnable tools and permissions; subagents/remote coding CLIs are not evidence of autonomous other VSM functions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No proven first-party S2-specific attenuation of concrete clashes among distinct viable S1 operational participants at assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential conflicting tool writes, duplicate requests and simultaneous subordinate work; the reviewed mechanisms bound a tool session and admission rather than negotiate interference among independent cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Parent model chooses when to invoke task or other tools; deterministic host approval/permission rules enforce per-request safety. - Decision owner: Task-local caller/model and mechanical permission/host mechanisms, with no evidenced distinct inter-unit adjustment owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-call sequential tool execution, subagent task API, session serialization/host state, tool approval and provider workroom state. - Closure path: Delegated subagent sends result back to caller; no demonstrated disturbance between two independent S1 units → conflict-sensitive coordination choice → returned stabilizing changes to their later operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Subagent dispatch and same-turn tool ordering are composition/scheduling, not a distinct operational S2 conflict attenuation loop. - Evidence: subagents.py; toolexecutor.py; providerworkroom.py; approval.py. - Basis: Reviewed installed delegation and execution coordination surfaces; bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high at defined distributed app boundary. - Caveats: Custom adopters can build their own cross-agent coordinator; that is not shown as an included native S2 operating mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No established whole-current aggregate control loop across independently executing Agent operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task backlogs, concurrent hosted sessions, scheduled work, tool costs and competing commitments that could require system-level current prioritization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Individual task model sets steps/tools; host scheduler executes author-specified entries; approval hook enforces permissions. No app-scoped present-wide resource/commitment decision-right shown. - Decision owner: Per-agent model, deterministic hosted scheduler and external operator setting static job rules; no demonstrated S3 owner or parent S3 adjudication/return cycle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TodoList, per-agent max iterations, stop handling, workroom run/trace, schedule-state and status views, host deployment. - Closure path: Per-task execution and scheduling have operational completion receipts, but no whole-current work-state integration → binding cross-S1 prioritization/commitment negotiation → changed subsequent agent operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An autonomous per-task plan is not S3 at this recursion, nor does a cron dispatcher become an organizational current-control decision-maker. - Evidence: agent.py; schedule.py; todolist.py; providerworkroom.py; agent.py. - Basis: Source-inspected runtime, scheduling and host responsibilities; bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high for default/packaged modes. - Caveats: External owner may use manual controls for their larger organization; not credited as a ConnectOnion-shipped parent return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Complementary independent challenge of ordinary operating reality returning into current control was not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent&#39;s potentially misleading self-report of completed work and inaccurate tool outcomes; optional eval can score such reports but is not wired into current control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Optional eval computes post-turn expected vs observed summary and LLM pass/fail; writes an evaluation UI/trace event, with no evidenced independent authority to challenge an S3 claim and force current operational correction. - Decision owner: Extra optional scorer model plus caller choosing plugin, separated from required first-party operating control; it is not demonstrated an S3 operating auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Turn trace, summarization, oncomplete handler, expected-outcome prompt, UI eval event; debug/auto-debug instrumentation. - Closure path: Agent turn completes → optional post-turn judge reads requested intent/actions/response → records and displays evaluation. Missing return: independent finding → accountable whole-current S3 reconsideration/withholding/corrective decision → changed S1 operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model grading completed task quality is genuine evaluation but its stored output is not a complementary governance audit with closed corrective challenge of current-control reporting. - Evidence: eval.py; eval.py; eval.py; agent.py; agent.py. - Basis: Explicit optional eval handler, separate post-task output and default factory plugin list inspection. - Confidence: Medium-high in evaluated native paths. - Caveats: Adopters can read UI evaluation and choose manual corrective interventions externally; this is not an evidenced built-in S3 return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Plausible first-party prospective capability creation via model-selected skill authoring, but an accountable future-adaptation decision plus return into subsequently installed operation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New automation sites, changed external interfaces, recurring tool workflows and a need for durable reusable future capabilities beyond the current task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The shipped model-backed co ai can write files and invoke skills; packaged browser-workflow-skill-builder instructs a user/agent to create a new SKILL.md under project skills and test helper scripts. Who accepts/promotes a candidate as a system future strategy/capability and when it becomes active is not fully established. - Decision owner: Model-assisted skill authoring under user tool permissions; first-party skills loader discovers/loads available skills. The prospective parent/operator selection and systematic future-control authority remain unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: File tools, developer-selected skills, skill invocation, project skill discovery, runtime prompt instructions and tool approval. - Closure path: Novel task/site issue → optionally invoke supplied skill-builder recipe → model/file tools create or edit a project skill → loader may discover and invoke this artifact on a later task. Missing: evidenced S4-specific option comparison/selection by legitimate future intelligence owner and a returned institutional decision admitting a new capability to future operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Writing durable skill code under a prompted task may be ordinary S1 programming work; a usable skills loader alone is not proof that the agent exercises a distinct S4 future/environment adaptation right. Material shipped candidate precludes an unqualified absence conclusion. - Evidence: agent.py; skills.py; skills.py; skills.py; SKILL.md; SKILL.md; agent.py. - Basis: Specific shipped skills authoring, file I/O and future load path, but missing conclusive S4 decision/return reconstruction. - Confidence: High for authoring/availability mechanics; low for whole S4 ownership state. - Caveats: Need a source-verified or controlled reproduction tying the model&#39;s prospective selection, durable skill admission and later changed operating options to one first-party S4 function, separated from ordinary user-requested coding.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate ultimate-purpose/organizational-identity decision and returned policy closure established, despite trust and tool authorization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Untrusted remote clients, tool risk, provider delegation and access-right changes; these are operational admission/safety disturbances, not by themselves ultimate identity disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: TrustAgent applies configured allow/deny rules and optionally a model &#39;ask&#39; for unknown remote clients; owner/operator approves tool execution. Nothing in these paths defines and adjudicates the installed agent&#39;s ultimate purpose or ethos on behalf of its governed organization. - Decision owner: Operator-configured trust/permission policies, per-request user, deterministic gate and bounded trust model decision for admission; not an established S5 organizational parent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Signed client identity/trust levels, trusted-client onboarding, tool approval modes, permission scopes and audit/trace. - Closure path: Client request → trust allow/deny, or tool action → permission/approval → execution/blocked outcome; no distinct ultimate identity/purpose dispute → legitimate parent decision → accepted revised governing policy affecting later operations was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An autonomous classifier of access to an endpoint is not the final legitimate guardian of an organization&#39;s identity; self-hosted owner may provide that authority externally. - Evidence: trustagent.py; trustagent.py; approval.py; skills.py; agent.py. - Basis: Explicit first-party trust and permissions control plus boundary-relative negative adjudication. - Confidence: Medium-high for selected packaged modes. - Caveats: Self-hosted operators retain ultimate external governance of what this agent is for; no first-party parent-mode closure is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/08bcb42e2953ad3304dc3b6275958bf2480c96ad/assessments/connectonion.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 08bcb42e2953ad3304dc3b6275958bf2480c96ad\">08bcb42 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T18:43:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ovh/shai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SHAI</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ovh/shai/tree/f076f6128a826a96a28e47d0d674284b1552905e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f076f6128a826a96a28e47d0d674284b1552905e\">f076f61 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding work by selecting and revising model/tool actions against the live project environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, changing file state, command failures, incomplete context, model/tool errors, permission requirements and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next reasoning/tool action, interpret returned evidence, continue or pause, and revise subsequent work until the coding request is complete or terminated. - Decision owner: the active model-backed SHAI Brain. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent state machine, coder prompt/runner, filesystem/shell/fetch/todo/MCP tools, claim manager, controller protocol, project-context loading and provider adapters. - Closure path: user/project context → model chooses action/tool → tool or environment returns evidence → evidence is appended to the trace → model chooses the next action until terminal completion, pause or cancellation. - Boundary reachability: interactive CLI, headless mode and HTTP sessions all instantiate the repository-owned agent core/coder runner directly; no adjacent development workflow is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves the state machine, tools and controller but removes the open-ended coding decisions that turn observations into subsequent actions. - Evidence: README; shai-core/src/agent/README.md; shai-core/src/agent/agent.rs; shai-core/src/agent/brain.rs; shai-core/src/runners/coder/coder.rs; built-in tool implementations. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external providers supply inference and MCP services may add tools, but their internal behavior is not credited to SHAI.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 coordination function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent tool calls and multiple HTTP sessions exist, but they are not evidenced as distinct S1 units whose mutual interference is regulated through a coordination loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific decision over a concrete inter-S1 conflict or oscillation was found. - Decision owner: not established at S2 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: async tool aggregation, per-session controller locking, session IDs and request serialization. - Closure path: these mechanisms serialize or isolate work inside individual sessions; they do not close a peer coordination relation among distinct operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrency and session isolation are implementation mechanisms, not evidence of S2 by themselves. - Evidence: agent architecture README; shai-core/src/agent/agent.rs; shai-http/src/session/session.rs; shai-http/src/session/manager.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: separately launched SHAI agents could be composed externally, but no first-party coordination relation among them is supplied in the frozen standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: session cancellation, permission handling and a configured maximum session count constrain current execution, but they do not provide substantive whole-system resource/commitment management. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate actor observes the whole set of current operations and makes discretionary choices over shared priorities, resources, commitments or interventions on behalf of the whole. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HTTP session map, static maxsessions limit, per-session controller lock, cancel/state protocol and permission handling. - Closure path: the session manager deterministically accepts a new session when below a configured count and rejects it when full; session-local controller actions affect only the focal agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the hard concurrency cap enforces a configured limit but does not supply the whole-current decision authority required for S3. - Evidence: shai-http/src/session/manager.rs; shai-http/src/session/session.rs; shai-core/src/agent/protocol.rs; shai-core/src/agent/claims.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an external service operator may choose configuration and terminate sessions, but generic operational administration is not a first-party S3 closure at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no complementary audit path distinct from ordinary production feedback is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shell output, tool results, traces and repository tests can reveal mistakes, but the same coding agent consumes these as ordinary operational evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate auditor owns an independent claim-checking judgment that returns findings into current control. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool result events, trace logging, shell/build execution, repository tests and HTTP event streams. - Closure path: execution evidence returns directly into the same agent trace or external client; no separate complementary-access audit loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routine self-checking and observability do not establish S3. - Evidence: shai-core/src/agent/events.rs; built-in tools; agent output/logging; repository tests; HTTP event logging. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users may independently inspect results, but no first-party reviewer/evaluator runtime closes that path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persistent traces, project SHAI.md, custom agent configs, provider selection and MCP configuration can influence later sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party process senses future/external change, develops adaptation options for SHAI and returns a selected option into current organizational capability. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session persistence, project context files, custom-agent configuration, provider/model selection and MCP configuration. - Closure path: stored traces/configuration are replayed or loaded into later operational runs; they do not form an external-and-prospective adaptation conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence and extensibility change context/capabilities only when supplied by users/configuration and do not autonomously generate future-oriented adaptation options. - Evidence: README; SHAI.md; shai-http/src/session/persist.rs; shai-core/src/config/agent.rs; MCP configuration. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external operators can create new agent configurations, but generic configurability is not S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool claims, sudo mode, permission requests, agent configuration and external controller commands constrain ordinary task execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a durable governing decision for SHAI. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ClaimManager, sudo/no-sudo mode, permission protocol, custom-agent config and user/project context. - Closure path: permissions and controller commands allow or deny current actions, but do not settle identity-level policy questions for subsequent system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: guardrails and ordinary approval are execution constraints, not S5. - Evidence: shai-core/src/agent/claims.rs; shai-core/src/agent/agent.rs; protocol/configuration surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: maintainers and service operators have governance authority outside the focal runtime, but that adjacent authority is not an operational S5 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/08e55e86d5171d1f261ba0ecba7dd97b9e65b5be/assessments/shai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 08e55e86d5171d1f261ba0ecba7dd97b9e65b5be\">08e55e8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T01:19:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bubbuild/bub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bub</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bubbuild/bub/tree/71d7dbf82f9e7145aabf53ab562c1d376bb3e0e2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 71d7dbf82f9e7145aabf53ab562c1d376bb3e0e2\">71d7dbf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomously complete assigned tasks via model-selected tool actions and feedback-informed next model steps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User goals, tool-return data, execution errors, changing session messages and provider responses require task-specific action selection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses content and offered tool invocations; the first-party agent executes chosen calls and exposes the results to subsequent model decisions. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed Agent/BubAgent work cell; the external inference service supplies decisions through a deliberately integrated provider interface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tape/history, tool call executor, bounded model steps, events, per-session state, skills, configured permissions and optional subtask call. - Closure path: BubAgent.runstream → Agent.start/runsteps → runonce/model streaming → model-selected tool call → ToolExecutor and tool result → recorded tape/model context → next step or final output. - Boundary reachability: BubAgent and core Agent are concrete installable first-party runtime classes; normal configured operation reaches this loop without an application-defined replacement agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The tool executor, session handler and fixed step caps cannot themselves select a semantic next action from task/observation content. The configured model-backed work cell does. - Evidence: implemented step loop; model streaming; configured turn; first-party subtask. - Basis: Structural source inspection of the first-party model/tool/result feedback loop and published standard runtime. - Confidence: High for implemented decision loop; no live-model efficacy/safety claim. - Caveats: Requires a configured model and runtime tools. A standalone subtask is not automatically a separately viable management layer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No shipped conflict-specific regulator is evidenced that detects and attenuates disturbances between two independently operating S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two sessions/subtasks may compete for shared tools, write to common resources or generate inconsistent proposals; first-party path does not detect or settle such cross-agent interference. - Distinct S1 units: Separate configured BubAgent instances or task runs can host model-backed loops; the subagent tool uses the parent&#39;s own runtime, only changing the session/tool scope. - Inter-S1 disturbance: No concrete peer-conflict signal, oscillation detector, ownership collision or resource contention reconciliation is found in the shipped subagent/admission path. - Attenuating coordination relation: Channel admission affects new messages and session activity; subagent delegation waits for an output; tool executor gathers concurrent calls without a shared-work conflict policy. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Model can read delegated text/error results as ordinary work input, but no inter-S1 conflict outcome is fed back under a dedicated coordination decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Message admission and worker handoff are mechanisms that a larger S2 might use, not proof of a particular collision or disagreement and its mitigation. Explicit first-party default concurrency test confirms admission is not a universal serializing inter-agent guard. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/plugin provides optional admission policy; first-party code applies AdmitDecision, not an inter-agent reconciliation rule. - Decision owner: No qualified S2 owner in the reviewed first-party boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session pending queue, steering inbox, quit cancellation, model tool allowlist, optional tool concurrency limiter, subagent prompt/result. - Closure path: Incoming session message → optional admission decision → process/drop/queue/steer; parent subtask call → child output. Neither closes detected inter-S1 interference into revised multiple-agent work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-originated delegation can be autonomous S1 task decomposition, but the admission actions are externally provided or single-session controls, not an agent-owned cross-S1 stabilization function. - Evidence: subagent call and result; admission decision actions; concurrent tool calls; default concurrent turns test. - Basis: Bounded negative on first-party multi-run and channel interfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A consumer-supplied admission hook could build an inter-agent policy; absent concrete shipped ownership, it is not credited to Bub.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party manager appraises the current whole of active operational units and revises their shared task commitments, resource allocations or priorities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent sessions and subtask work could consume capacity or drift from higher-level objectives, but the standard controls manage one run or a fixed session queue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The operator supplies tasks/permissions/models and may interrupt a session; a model can choose to call a child for its own task but cannot, through the shipped subagent tool, inspect/rebalance an active agent organization. - Decision owner: Caller/developer for population topology and process settings; one operational parent model for task delegation, not an established separate whole-current manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChannelManager.activetasks, turn snapshots, quit, tool budgets/maxsteps, parent/child session IDs and optional hooks. - Closure path: One parent awaits subtask output; individual sessions can be interrupted or queued. No whole-current appraisal → commitment/resource choice → changed operating-unit priorities exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses whether to decompose its present request, but that decision remains S1 production without current-organization visibility or binding portfolio-level resource rights. - Evidence: subagent single-run invocation; session-specific quit and active task list; bounded local agent steps. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting apparent parent-control affordances. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A custom app can assemble an S3 using the hooks and child results; that would be a different organizational boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently sourced audit of operational claims that returns a corrective finding to a distinct current-control owner is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model or tool outputs may be wrong; tape events, tool failures and optional hooks expose the producer&#39;s own record, not independent verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator/plugins may inspect traces or reject tool calls, but the first-party runtime has no separate auditor deciding the credibility of S1 reports against independent ground truth. - Decision owner: None for an S3-qualified audit channel; producer agent owns task output and caller owns external tests. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tape entries, tracing, beforetoolcall and aftertoolcall, event subscriptions, error notifications and SDK test fixtures. - Closure path: Producer&#39;s tool result/event is logged and fed to its own loop; no separate independent evidence check → audit opinion → corrective S3 control return is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Trace creation and permission checks are deterministic observability/execution controls, not an autonomous independent challenge to production claims. - Evidence: built-in tool hook; agent step events; tool traces. - Basis: Bounded negative first-party production and observation review. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Future custom reviewer/plugin functionality should be assessed at its own operating boundary, with separate evidence rights.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party loop senses prospective outside changes, selects future organizational adaptations and returns adopted capability changes to present operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model/tool/provider shifts and emerging user needs may require future changes, but the runtime responds to current tasks and loads operator-configured capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operators select model, tools and skills; model calls/retries within a task have only current-request authority. - Decision owner: Developer/operator for installed capacity and configuration, not a dedicated prospective S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model selection, skill discovery, deferred tool loading, plugin registration, setmodel, context handoff and web fetching as a task tool. - Closure path: Operator changes tools/provider/skills and runs again; present-task fetch/feedback can inform next local answer but not strategic adoption based on future-environment appraisal. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An S1 may research a future-facing question, but its output is not a first-party future capability governance cycle. - Evidence: agent skill/tool scope; built-in tool/provider controls; local context recovery. - Basis: Bounded negative on operating, capability and extension surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A larger organization may separately build environmental intelligence with these tools; standard Bub does not evidence that circuit.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy governance channel with legitimate authority, escalation and binding return is instantiated by the shipped system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe tools, unexpected user messages and forbidden capabilities are addressed by first-party hooks, allowlists and operator configuration, not identity-level constitutional arbitration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operators configure tool/model access; plugin tool-call decisions can block/rewrite individual calls. No distinguished S5 holder adjudicates conflicting mission/identity rules and returns binding system policy. - Decision owner: Outside operator/developer for application policy; model work cell for task-level responses, not S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: allowedtools/allowedskills, beforetoolcall interception, ToolCallDecision, CLI quit and channel admission hooks. - Closure path: A disallowed tool call can be refused or a message queued/dropped; nothing escalates a normative identity dispute to a legitimate final authority with durable policy revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Configured constraints operate within S1 tasks; an interactive user or plugin is not automatically a defined constitutional authority. - Evidence: turn capability scope; tool interception; channel admission. - Basis: Bounded negative on first-party operator and hook policy interfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External deployment governance cannot be imported as runtime S5 merely because a human operator exists.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0298deac01c97c8eb11fdede4a23ff799d679fe9/assessments/bu/bub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0298deac01c97c8eb11fdede4a23ff799d679fe9\">0298dea &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T16:12:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ishaan1013/shadow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shadow</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ishaan1013/shadow/tree/96e7b187951d66740dcd7d0bf3d5f94f5d09ed41\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 96e7b187951d66740dcd7d0bf3d5f94f5d09ed41\">96e7b18 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work in a task-scoped repository workspace and deliver resulting changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, repository structure, file/Git state, tool/terminal/search output, provider responses, implementation failures, context/history and changing task evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which files/evidence to inspect, what terminal/search/edit action to execute, how to revise work from tool results, what TODO/memory action to take and when to stop responding. - Decision owner: the model-backed Shadow coding agent in the first-party streamText multi-step loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party tool schemas/executors; task/workspace initialization; local/remote execution abstraction; TODOs; repository memory; semantic search; task status; checkpoints/history; Git commit/push and PR delivery. - Closure path: task + workspace/session context → model chooses a tool/action → Shadow executes it → result is returned inside the same multi-step model stream → model chooses subsequent action or completes → Shadow persists/delivers resulting workspace changes. - Boundary reachability: normal Shadow task execution directly wires the model to built-in coding tools with maxSteps: 100; no downstream orchestration code is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor leaves workspace/tool/storage/delivery machinery but removes the open-ended coding judgment that selects and sequences work. - Evidence: stream-processor.ts; tools/index.ts; chat.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference is provider-backed; Shadow is credited for the first-party agent/tool/feedback organization and execution boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material same-recursion inter-S1 coordination function was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S2 level. Shadow isolates task workspaces, but reviewed evidence does not establish distinct production S1 units inside one task organization whose mutual interference is specifically attenuated by a first-party coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task workspaces/branches; Kata/QEMU isolation; task initialization/status; queued task actions. - Closure path: not applicable; no distinct-S1 interference → coordination response → changed subsequent S1 behavior loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: workspace isolation is primarily task/environment containment. Independent hosted tasks are not promoted into one organization without evidence of a shared same-recursion operational objective and interference relation. - Evidence: README.md; chat.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a larger deployment could coordinate multiple Shadow tasks externally; that organization is outside this task-level assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-organization current-control function was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task status, initialization state, active streams, queued actions, cleanup, workspace availability and background services are tracked, but these are lifecycle/runtime controls around the focal task rather than whole-system operational management. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task statuses; active stream/cancel/queue maps; initialization engine; cleanup scheduling; background service manager; TODO state; Git/PR state. - Closure path: not applicable; no whole-system current view plus substantive organization-wide resource/priority/commitment intervention loop was reconstructed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model owns coding decisions inside S1; task lifecycle state and scheduling are deterministic/operator-driven mechanisms. - Evidence: chat.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: real-time task tracking is operationally useful but does not by itself satisfy the stronger S3 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit actor/path distinct from the producing S1 was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary tool errors, command results and generated PR metadata can expose problems, but no separate reviewer independently audits the producing agent&#39;s correctness/completion claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: command/tool validation; Git diff/commit generation; PR snapshot creation; task status; ordinary model self-observation. - Closure path: not applicable; no producing-S1 claim → complementary independent evidence path → audit verdict → corrective return loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: command/security validation is deterministic enforcement and PR generation summarizes/delivers changes; neither is a separate autonomous complementary auditor. - Evidence: chat.ts; pull-requests.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can review the draft PR externally; that external review is not a first-party autonomous S3 path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material outside-and-future intelligence/adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository-specific knowledge can persist across tasks through memories, and semantic/wiki context can improve later operation, but no explicit prospective environmental-modeling and adaptation-selection function was reconstructed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: addmemory, listmemories, removememory; automatic memory injection into later prompts; Shadow Wiki; optional semantic indexing; persisted task history. - Closure path: not applicable; no external/future distinction → adaptation-option generation → autonomous selection → persistent organizational capability/strategy change → later operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coding agent may decide to save useful facts, and those facts are later reused, but remembering operational knowledge is not itself the stronger prospective adaptation function. - Evidence: tools/index.ts; memory-service.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: persistence is substantial; the negative classification is specifically about S4&#39;s prospective adaptation closure, not about whether Shadow learns/reuses repository facts colloquially.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: custom rules, command security, workspace boundaries, user settings, model/provider choice and auto-PR settings constrain operation, but they are task/runtime policy rather than identity-level governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: user/deployment/project configuration for relevant constraints; deterministic Shadow mechanisms enforce those selections. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: custom rules; command validation; workspace path protection; user settings; model selection; local/remote mode; auto-PR option. - Closure path: not applicable at S5 level; no identity/policy conflict → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned organizational operation loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coding model does not own Shadow&#39;s ultimate policy/identity boundary, and enforcement of configured restrictions is not itself S5. - Evidence: README.md; chat.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a broader organization may govern Shadow through external policy; that parent is not imported without a function-specific closed loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0127536997698de17d4e0decbd3baf4cf3f3d46e/assessments/shadow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0127536997698de17d4e0decbd3baf4cf3f3d46e\">0127536 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T17:27:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SWE-agent/mini-swe-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mini-swe-agent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SWE-agent/mini-swe-agent/tree/04d809ceab9df28f9adaed044884180159172930\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 04d809ceab9df28f9adaed044884180159172930\">04d809c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Direct first-party model/tool turn loop performs a coding task against a local or container execution environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain task request, shell/tool errors, changing workspace files and model responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select environment commands/actions from current model context and revise after observed results. - Decision owner: Model-driven DefaultAgent (or InteractiveAgent inheriting it) makes task-specific execution choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model.query, Environment.execute, Jinja templates, observation messages, exit/step/time/cost bounds. - Closure path: Task and prompt history → model proposes actions → environment executes → returned observations → next model query or stop. - Boundary reachability: First-party CLI entrypoint constructs DefaultAgent/InteractiveAgent and invokes run; actual shell/environment execution occurs through executeactions, not a toy graph. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task-specific model choice drives actual environment operations; deterministic limits and environment runners are supporting mechanisms. - Evidence: src/minisweagent/agents/default.py; src/minisweagent/environments/local.py; src/minisweagent/models/litellmmodel.py; src/minisweagent/run/mini.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation at the declared single-agent distribution boundary. - Distinct S1 units: Only the one current DefaultAgent/InteractiveAgent is evidenced; external separate invocations are not owned subagents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: No shipped interacting worker fleet whose conflicts/oscillations require coordination. - Attenuating coordination relation: No joint-work arbitration, conflict feedback or external worker resource rights found. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Own action observations feed only the same S1 agent. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: A single model turn/action loop is not an S2 coordination relation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential multi-agent content/file collisions are absent as a coordinated first-party operating mode; a user can separately launch processes outside this one-agent recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No inter-worker arbitration right among distinct operating agents is implemented. - Decision owner: No S2 owner at the declared one-agent boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One model/agent and one Environment; step loop serially dispatches selected commands. - Closure path: Only a single agent&#39;s actions and observations return to itself; no cross-agent conflict or corrected coordination path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No first-party function-specific higher-level owner or full closure exists in the inspected supported single-agent modes. - Evidence: src/minisweagent/agents/default.py; src/minisweagent/agents/interactive.py; src/minisweagent/run/mini.py; src/minisweagent/environments/local.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separate whole-current operational director controlling a plurality of independently acting agents or resource commitments. - Whole-system current view: Only one agent&#39;s messages, execution count, cost, wall time and command observations. - Current-control decision scope: Individual command/turn limits, not team resources and commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task step/time/cost state and human command confirmations apply to one running task rather than team portfolio. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model selects the next operating step, while static limits terminate; no above-S1 resource bargaining/priority intervention. - Decision owner: None established at a higher team recursion; the single operating agent owns task-local decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: steplimit, costlimit, walltimelimitseconds, InteractiveAgent prompt and parent process. - Closure path: Step cost/errors → terminate or next action of same S1; no independent worker reports → whole-system control → modified shared commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No first-party function-specific higher-level owner or full closure exists in the inspected supported single-agent modes. - Evidence: src/minisweagent/agents/default.py; src/minisweagent/agents/interactive.py; src/minisweagent/run/mini.py; src/minisweagent/config/default.yaml - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate, sufficiently independent complementary inspection of operational reality challenging produced task claims in the bundled agent. - Claim being audited: Model&#39;s code/task completion claim. - Ordinary reporting path: Model messages and its own environment command output. - Complementary access path: No independent source/artifact observation owner, only ordinary tool observations. - Independence boundary: Separate reviewer or audit process unavailable in standard distribution. - Who acts on findings: None; ordinary tool errors alter the same agent&#39;s next actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect task completion/code is not separately audited by a first-party observer; ordinary command feedback remains in S1. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct audit sampling/judgment right beyond ordinary model/tool observations and optional human confirmation of commands. - Decision owner: None; model observes its own executed commands and optional user approves individual actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Command stdout/stderr and exit observations, serialized trajectory and ordinary task feedback. - Closure path: Tool execution output goes to its own producing model, not independently sampled and judged with corrective control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No first-party function-specific higher-level owner or full closure exists in the inspected supported single-agent modes. - Evidence: src/minisweagent/agents/default.py; src/minisweagent/agents/interactive.py; src/minisweagent/environments/local.py; src/minisweagent/run/mini.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party outside-and-prospective organizational capability adaptation loop beyond immediate coding task work. - External distinction: User task and model-supplied context are not ongoing external environmental horizon sensing. - Future / prospective distinction: Serialized trajectories and static templates alone are not future capability redesign. - Adaptation option generated: No qualifying organizational future-adaptation option. - Path back into current capability / S3: No separate capability-changing return beyond ordinary new prompt. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future environment opportunity/threat and system redesign are outside this task-bounded loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent can adapt the next command, but cannot select and apply a future organizational capability adaptation in a shipped separate function. - Decision owner: No prospective S4 authority; user chooses static model, environment and configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Config YAML, model provider wrapper, task conversation and saved trajectory. - Closure path: Messages inform the current task only; no external future observation → adaptation option → changed operational capability loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No first-party function-specific higher-level owner or full closure exists in the inspected supported single-agent modes. - Evidence: src/minisweagent/agents/default.py; src/minisweagent/models/litellmmodel.py; src/minisweagent/config/default.yaml; src/minisweagent/run/mini.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate organizational identity/policy governance loop in the single-agent standard distribution. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Who chooses and revises overarching organization purpose and authority. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Outside user, not an operational first-party parent governance loop. - Return-to-operation path: Action confirmation governs one command, not ultimate organizational policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Command approvals, tool limits and user-chosen settings bound local execution, not the systemic identity/purpose of an agent organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User may approve/deny an individual action and set static modes, not govern ultimate purpose via an operational returned decision loop. - Decision owner: User/developer external to the assessed single-agent organization; no first-party parent S5 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: InteractiveAgent human/confirm/yolo mode and regular-expression allowlist, task template, execution cost and step limits. - Closure path: Command action → user allow/deny → one action executes or cancels; no ultimate purpose/identity review and return that governs an organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No first-party function-specific higher-level owner or full closure exists in the inspected supported single-agent modes. - Evidence: src/minisweagent/agents/interactive.py; src/minisweagent/config/default.yaml; src/minisweagent/agents/default.py; src/minisweagent/run/mini.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b20dddb7930735d5399471b0299ec0054f57df35/assessments/mini-swe-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b20dddb7930735d5399471b0299ec0054f57df35\">b20dddb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T23:16:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/basnijholt/agent-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent CLI</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/basnijholt/agent-cli/tree/554344e6ed5266bcc2a7b1461d4c84744ff5e0af\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 554344e6ed5266bcc2a7b1461d4c84744ff5e0af\">554344e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-selected conversational tool use and, in dev mode, task-local autonomous coding by a managed external coding-agent process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended natural-language tasks, file information gaps, command outcomes, search/memory feedback, implementation uncertainty and tool failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select available tools and their arguments in response to a request/results, refine subsequent steps and return an answer or task result. - Decision owner: the PydanticAI chat model for the first-party conversational S1; alternatively an installed external coding-agent executable launched as a supported dev worker for its own task. No external agent&#39;s hidden organizational metasystem is credited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider selection, ASR and TTS, createllmagent, agent.run, file/shell/search/memory Tool wrappers, saved history; optional spawned process and configured branch/worktree. - Closure path: user request/transcribed text → first-party model-backed agent chooses tool actions → local tools return real data/error → agent continues/responds → output/history is recorded; in dev mode assigned prompt → launched coding agent uses its task loop in designated checkout → branch/result becomes available to the user/external caller. - Boundary reachability: agent-cli chat constructs a PydanticAI Agent with first-party tools, calls agent.run, and is a documented installable command; dev new --agent codex --prompt-file ... and alternatives are supported launch paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-backed chat agent chooses which file/search/command tool to invoke; command implementations and audio transport alone are not the agent. Optional coding workers independently choose implementation actions, while Agent CLI only starts them. - Evidence: PydanticAI service, chat model call, tool registry, dev launch. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for source-path reachability; medium for external coding agent task performance. - Caveats: no actual provider-backed voice/chat/coding task was executed for assessment; nonagentic transcription and static text transformations alone are not the positive S1 evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: deterministic checkout isolation between distinct concurrently executing dev-mode coding agents on a shared repository. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independent installed coding agents launched into separate branch-specific worktrees of one source repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent changes to the same working directory, git index or checked-out branch can conflict, overwrite pending changes or corrupt assumptions of another S1 process. - Attenuating coordination relation: agent-cli dev new provisions a separate git branch/worktree and launches the corresponding agent in that worktree; each later coding action addresses its dedicated mutable checkout, rather than a shared git index/working directory. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the new worktree path is passed as the agent&#39;s process working directory; subsequent reads/writes/git commands operate on that isolated state. Worktree creation failures are returned to caller instead of launching an agent in an unintended shared location. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: worktree provisioning regulates the concrete collision type of simultaneous checkout/index/file mutations by distinct coding agents. It is not scored because the tool can spawn agents or list sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mutable repository working-tree interference during parallel code changes, branch-switch side effects and git index contention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party CLI constructs a separate branch/checkout and chooses to execute the worker there after caller configuration. - Decision owner: human/external parent selects whether/where to parallelize; deterministic Agent CLI code performs isolation (C). Agent CLI has no agent-owned inter-worker conflict judgment/negotiation loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dev new, createworktree, git worktree add -b, branch-name validation, optional prompt file and cwd-scoped launchagent, status/remove commands. - Closure path: caller assigns different tasks/branches → Agent CLI creates separate worktree paths → separate S1 coding agents execute in those paths → direct shared-index/write collisions are attenuated while each worker returns its own result/branch for external integration. - Boundary reachability: the normal installable dev new ... --prompt or --prompt-file path invokes worktree.createworktree and launchagent; README, dev guide and packaged skill explicitly document multiple independent coding tasks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this isolation occurs because worktree provision is coded and selected through CLI flags; the spawned model agent need not detect/correct inter-worker conflicts. No adaptive S2 owner is shown, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: dev guide, dev CLI, worktree creation, agent launch, shipped skill. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the opt-in separation mechanism. - Caveats: separate worktrees do not automatically resolve logical changes, integration/rebase conflicts or task dependencies. The documented shared-worktree same-branch mode deliberately lacks this isolation; no positive S2 claim is made for that mode.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-dev-fleet adaptive control right with owned current operational view/return was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple agent tasks may compete for current attention, require reprioritization after a failed branch, or need worker reassignment and integration planning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no Agent CLI model/controller makes these whole-portfolio decisions; user or external Claude/Codex parent selects tasks and judges progress, while dev implements requested spawn/list/status/remove. - Decision owner: external invoking user/assistant or ordinary deterministic process manager, not an in-boundary first-party S3 agent/constructor/closed parent controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dev list, dev status, dev rm, launch hooks, optional tmux/zellij sessions, branch tracking, chat model loop and task-file records. - Closure path: each coding worker may produce a result visible in its checkout or report; a user/external caller must independently interpret all worker results and decide cross-workspace priorities/repair. The bundled dev CLI does not autonomously assemble the reports into whole-fleet current regulation. - Whole-system current view: CLI enumerates worktrees and git status for human/external consumption, not a shipped first-party adaptive controller. - Current-control decision scope: fixed lifecycle/cleanup and CLI-selected per-worktree operations; no evidenced internal authority over shared mission commitments or corrective portfolio dispatch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the shipped skill can tell another assistant how to spawn agents, but that assistant is outside the declared Agent CLI management boundary. Listing many sessions or putting instructions in task files is not itself whole-system S3 ownership. - Evidence: dev status/list, dev CLI, bundled skill, launch implementation. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a separate Claude Code or other parent assistant may be deliberately used to orchestrate the dev command. Its own current-control rights would require an assessment of that assembled parent system, not silent promotion of Agent CLI.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary, sufficiently independent agent audit with returned corrective or acceptance authority was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect coding-agent completion reports, unsound branch diffs or missed testing failures could require challenge independent of the implementing worker. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no bundled Agent CLI reviewer role autonomously obtains worker claims plus independent source/test access and issues an acceptance/fix veto into the dev integration path. - Decision owner: no qualifying first-party owner. User and an external agent may optionally inspect branches or spawn separately prompted reviewer agents, but no specific independent audit/merge-gate lifecycle is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate worktrees, optional review-specific user prompts, dev run output inspection, task report files, test tools and memory retrieval. - Closure path: users/outer coordinators can request an ad hoc review and decide what to do; dev itself neither mandates independent fresh review nor refuses integration on an audit verdict and returns failed claims for rework. - Claim being audited: hypothetical coding worker claim that a task branch implements the prompt correctly. - Ordinary reporting path: coding worker&#39;s output, prompt/task file and optional .claude/REPORT.md produced at the parent&#39;s request. - Complementary access path: not established as a first-party standard role; launching a second generic agent with a review prompt creates an optional external application, not a supplied complementary audit decision lifecycle. - Independence boundary: no built-in reviewer identity, separable evidence mandate, adjudication protocol, or merge-bound independent judgment is enforced at the declared boundary. - Who acts on findings: human/external orchestrator would choose corrections; no first-party report→audit verdict→S1 correction loop is demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel execution and review-shaped prompts cannot by themselves establish an independent epistemic audit owner or acceptance gate. - Evidence: dev skill review examples, dev guide, dev status, agent launch. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this does not assert external coding agents or a manually configured reviewer cannot test code; it limits the claimed first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct external/prospective environmental-intelligence and capability-adaptation closure was evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing model/provider ecosystem, future development-tool needs, user preferences and long-horizon task requirements could demand new capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an agent/constructor/closed parent selecting new future-facing organizational capabilities based on external sensing and returning them to the present Agent CLI runtime. - Decision owner: none established for a qualifying S4 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory fact extraction and reconciliation, RAG retrieval, conversational web search, AI branch names, model/provider configuration, optional extras/install-services and local background service management. - Closure path: memory summaries and facts can influence subsequent conversation content; optional package/plugin/provider changes are requested by an operator. No first-party external/future scanning → capability option generation → authorized adaptation into current organizational control loop was identified. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reconciling contradictory user memories, answering a web query or auto-selecting a branch name is task/context support, not S4 organizational prospecting and adaptive capability return. - Evidence: memory architecture, memory client, chat tools, dev branch naming, config docs. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an external user-built memory/agent automation can implement future adaptation, but that would expand the system boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate identity/constitutional-policy decision lifecycle was evidenced at the Agent CLI installation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: human privacy intent, provider/network boundaries, safe tool use and deployment rights can shape operation, but they do not themselves constitute an ultimate policy organ. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for adjudicating the system&#39;s own ultimate purpose/identity and returning a ratified change into operations. - Decision owner: human installs/configures the tool, chooses local or remote model, and approves task/process choices; no distinct first-party S5 parent-governance mode with function-specific closure is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: privacy-focused README, provider settings, OS permissions, user agent prompts, dev hooks, tool execution scope, macOS menu controls, memory consent hints and service installation. - Closure path: these static/user-set constraints shape tools and provider access, but no identity dispute → authoritative policy judgment → returned organizational mandate cycle appears in shipped runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: privacy defaults and operator control express design and configuration, not a self-contained S5 decision right. An external user/organization may own S5 at a different recursion. - Evidence: README privacy boundary, configuration, tool module, dev hooks. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a parent organization using Agent CLI may have independent governance, not imported into this standalone assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/87f2678d714b39c7ddc9c457297f0f80052dd125/assessments/ag/agent-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 87f2678d714b39c7ddc9c457297f0f80052dd125\">87f2678 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
